window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2018-06-1", "source": "2018年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of building trust between employers and employees. You can cite examples to illustrate your views. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Trust is the invisible thread that holds a workplace together. In today's competitive business world, building\nmutual trust between employers and employees is not merely desirable but essential, for it shapes how\neffectively an organization performs and grows.\nOn the one hand, trust boosts efficiency and reduces internal friction. When employers trust their staff with\ngreater autonomy, employees feel respected and work with genuine initiative rather than fear. On the other\nhand, mutual trust strengthens loyalty and a sense of belonging. Take Google as an example: by granting\nengineers freedom to spend part of their time on personal projects, the company earned deep employee\ncommitment, and this trust in turn gave birth to innovations such as Gmail. Clearly, when both sides believe in\neach other, cooperation replaces suspicion.\nIn conclusion, trust between the two sides is the foundation of a healthy and productive workplace. Both\nemployers and employees should therefore communicate openly and keep their promises, so that trust can take\nroot and benefit everyone."}, "translation": {"prompt": "过去，拥有一辆私家车对大部分中国人而言是件奢侈的事。如今，私家车在中国随处可见。汽车成了人们生活中不可或缺的一部分，他们不仅开车上下班，还经常驾车出游。有些城市的汽车增长速度过快，以至于交通拥堵和停车位不足的问题日益严峻，这些城市的市政府不得不出台新规，限制上路汽车的数量。由于空气污染日益严重，现在越来越多的人选择购买新能源汽车，中国政府也采取了一些措施，支持新能源汽车的发展。", "sample": "In the past, it was a luxury for most Chinese people to own a private car. Nowadays, private cars can be seen everywhere in China. Cars have become an indispensable part of people's daily life; they not only drive to and from work but also often drive out for trips. In some cities, the number of cars grows so fast that traffic congestion and a shortage of parking spaces have become increasingly serious, so the municipal governments have to introduce new rules to limit the number of cars on the road. Due to the worsening air pollution, more and more people now choose to buy new energy vehicles, and the Chinese government has also taken some measures to support the development of new energy vehicles."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2018-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Scientists scanning and mapping the Giza pyramids say they've discovered that the Great Pyramid of Giza is not exactly even. But really not by much. This pyramid is the oldest of the world's Seven Wonders. The pyramid's exact size has  __26__  experts for centuries, as the \"more than 21 acres of hard, white casing stones\" that originally covered it were  __27__  long ago. Reporting in the most recent issue of the newsletter \"AERAGRAM,\" which  __28__  the work of the Ancient Egypt Research Associates, engineer Glen Dash says his team used a new measuring approach that involved finding any surviving  __29__  of the casing in order to determine where the original edge was. They found the east side of the pyramid to be a  __30__  of 5.5 inches shorter than the west side.\nThe question that most  __31__  him, however, isn't how the Egyptians who designed and built the pyramid got it wrong 4,500 years ago, but how they got it so close to  __32__  . \"We can only speculate as to how the Egyptians could have laid out these lines with such  __33__  using only the tools they had,\" Dash writes. He says his  __34__  is that the Egyptians laid out their design on a grid, noting that the Great Pyramid is oriented only  __35__  away from the cardinal directions (its north-south axis runs 3 minutes 54 seconds west of due north, while its east-west axis runs 3 minutes 51 seconds north of due east)—an amount that's \"tiny, but similar,\" archeologist Atlas Obscura points out.", "matchIntro": "Peer Pressure Has a Positive Side", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Parents of teenagers often view their children's friends with something like suspicion. They worry that the adolescent peer group has the power to push its members into behavior that is foolish and even dangerous. Such wariness is well founded: statistics show, for example, that a teenage driver with a same-age passenger in the car is at higher risk of a fatal crash than an adolescent driving alone or with an adult."}, {"label": "B", "text": "In a 2005 study, psychologist Laurence Steinberg of Temple University and his co-author, psychologist Margo Gardner, then at Temple, divided 306 people into three age groups: young adolescents, with a mean age of 14; older adolescents, with a mean age of 19; and adults, aged 24 and older. Subjects played a computerized driving game in which the player must avoid crashing into a wall that materializes, without warning, on the roadway. Steinberg and Gardner randomly assigned some participants to play alone or with two same-age peers looking on."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Older adolescents scored about 50 percent higher on an index of risky driving when their peers were in the room—and the driving of early adolescents was fully twice as reckless when other young teens were around. In contrast, adults behaved in similar ways regardless of whether they were on their own or observed by others. \"The presence of peers makes adolescents and youth, but not adults, more likely to take risks,\" Steinberg and Gardner concluded."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Yet in the years following the publication of this study, Steinberg began to believe that this interpretation did not capture the whole picture. As he and other researchers examined the question of why teens were more apt to take risks in the company of other teenagers, they came to suspect that a crowd's influence need not always be negative. Now some experts are proposing that we should take advantage of the teen brain's keen sensitivity to the presence of friends and leverage it to improve education."}, {"label": "E", "text": "In a 2011 study, Steinberg and his colleagues turned to functional MRI(磁共振) to investigate how the presence of peers affects the activity in the adolescent brain. They scanned the brains of 40 teens and adults who were playing a virtual driving game designed to test whether players would brake at a yellow light or speed on through the crossroad."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The brains of teenagers, but not adults, showed greater activity in two regions associated with rewards when they were being observed by same-age peers than when alone. In other words, rewards are more intense for teens when they are with peers, which motivates them to pursue higher-risk experiences that might bring a big payoff (such as the thrill of just making the light before it turns red). But Steinberg suspected this tendency could also have its advantages. In his latest experiment, published online in August, Steinberg and his colleagues used a computerized version of a card game called the Iowa Gambling Task to investigate how the presence of peers affects the way young people gather and apply information."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The results: Teens who played the Iowa Gambling Task under the eyes of fellow adolescents engaged in more exploratory behavior, learned faster from both positive and negative outcomes, and achieved better performance on the task than those who played in solitude. \"What our study suggests is that teenagers learn more quickly and more effectively when their peers are present than when they're on their own,\" Steinberg says. And this finding could have important implications for how we think about educating adolescents."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Matthew D. Lieberman, a social cognitive neuroscientist at the University of California, Los Angeles, and author of the 2013 book Social: Why Our Brains Are Wired to Connect, suspects that the human brain is especially skillful at learning socially significant information. He points to a classic 2004 study in which psychologists at Dartmouth College and Harvard University used functional MRI to track brain activity in 17 young men as they listened to descriptions of people while concentrating on either socially relevant cues (for example, trying to form an impression of a person based on the description) or more socially neutral information (such as noting the order of details in the description). The descriptions were the same in each condition, but people could better remember these statements when given a social motivation."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The study also found that when subjects thought about and later recalled descriptions in terms of their informational content, regions associated with factual memory, such as the medial temporal lobe, became active. But thinking about or remembering descriptions in terms of their social meaning activated the dorsomedial prefrontal cortex—part of the brain's social network—even as traditional memory regions registered low levels of activity. More recently, as he reported in a 2012 review, Lieberman has discovered that this region may be part of a distinct network involved in socially motivated learning and memory. Such findings, he says, suggest that \"this network can be called on to process and store the kind of information taught in school—potentially giving students access to a range of untapped mental powers.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "If humans are generally geared to recall details about one another, this pattern is probably even more powerful among teenagers who are very attentive to social details: who is in, who is out, who likes whom, who is mad at whom. Their desire for social drama is not—or not only—a way of distracting themselves from their schoolwork or of driving adults crazy. It is actually a neurological(神经的) sensitivity, initiated by hormonal changes. Evolutionarily speaking, people in this age group are at a stage in which they can prepare to find a mate and start their own family while separating from parents and striking out on their own. To do this successfully, their brain prompts them to think and even obsess about others."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Yet our schools focus primarily on students as individual entities. What would happen if educators instead took advantage of the fact that teens are powerfully compelled to think in social terms? In Social, Lieberman lays out a number of ways to do so. History and English could be presented through the lens of the psychological drives of the people involved. One could therefore present Napoleon in terms of his desire to impress or Churchill in terms of his lonely gloom. Less inherently interpersonal subjects, such as math, could acquire a social aspect through team problem solving and peer tutoring. Research shows that when we absorb information in order to teach it to someone else, we learn it more accurately and deeply, perhaps in part because we are engaging our social cognition."}, {"label": "L", "text": "And although anxious parents may not welcome the notion, educators could turn adolescent recklessness to academic ends. \"Risk taking in an educational context is a vital skill that enables progress and creativity,\" wrote Sarah-Jayne Blakemore, a cognitive neuroscientist at University College London, in a review published last year. Yet, she noted, many young people are especially unwilling to take risks at school—afraid that one low test score or poor grade could cost them a spot at a selective university. We should assure such students that risk, and even peer pressure, can be a good thing—as long as it happens in the classroom and not in the car."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The Ebro Delta, in Spain, famous as a battleground during the Spanish Civil War, is now the setting for a different contest, one that is pitting rice farmers against two enemies: the rice-eating giant apple snail, and rising sea levels. What happens here will have a bearing on the future of European rice production and the overall health of southern European wetlands.\nLocated on the Mediterranean just two hours south of Barcelona, the Ebro Delta produces 120 million kilograms of rice a year, making it one of the continent's most important rice-growing areas. As the sea creeps into these fresh-water marshes, however, rising salinity(盐分) is hampering rice production. At the same time, this sea-water also kills off the greedy giant apple snail, an introduced pest that feeds on young rice plants. The most promising strategy has become to harness one foe against the other.\nThe battle is currently being waged on land, in greenhouses at the University of Barcelona. Scientists working under the banner \"Project Neurice\" are seeking varieties of rice that can withstand the increasing salinity without losing the absorbency that makes European rice ideal for traditional Spanish and Italian dishes.\n\"The project has two sides,\" says Xavier Serrat, Neurice project manager and researcher at the University of Barcelona, \"the short-term fight against the snail—and a mid- to long-term fight against climate change. But the snail has given the project greater urgency.\"\nOriginally from South America, the snails were accidentally introduced into the Ebro Delta by Global Aquatic Technologies, a company that raised the snails for fresh-water aquariums(水族馆), but failed to prevent their escape. For now, the giant apple snail's presence in Europe is limited to the Ebro Delta. But the snail continues its march to new territory, says Serrat. \"The question is not whether it will reach other rice-growing areas of Europe, but when.\"\nOver the next year and a half investigators will test the various strains of salt-tolerant rice they've bred. In 2018, farmers will plant the varieties with the most promise in the Ebro Delta and Europe's other two main rice-growing regions—along the Po in Italy, and France's Rhone. A season in the field will help determine which, if any, of the varieties are ready for commercialization.\nAs an EU-funded effort, the search for salt-tolerant varieties of rice is taking place in all three countries. Each team is crossbreeding a local European short-grain rice with a long-grain Asian variety that carries the salt-resistant gene. The scientists are breeding successive generations to arrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance but retain about 97 percent of the European rice genome(基因组).", "47": "The Ebro Delta, in Spain, famous as a battleground during the Spanish Civil War, is now the setting for a different contest, one that is pitting rice farmers against two enemies: the rice-eating giant apple snail, and rising sea levels. What happens here will have a bearing on the future of European rice production and the overall health of southern European wetlands.\nLocated on the Mediterranean just two hours south of Barcelona, the Ebro Delta produces 120 million kilograms of rice a year, making it one of the continent's most important rice-growing areas. As the sea creeps into these fresh-water marshes, however, rising salinity(盐分) is hampering rice production. At the same time, this sea-water also kills off the greedy giant apple snail, an introduced pest that feeds on young rice plants. The most promising strategy has become to harness one foe against the other.\nThe battle is currently being waged on land, in greenhouses at the University of Barcelona. Scientists working under the banner \"Project Neurice\" are seeking varieties of rice that can withstand the increasing salinity without losing the absorbency that makes European rice ideal for traditional Spanish and Italian dishes.\n\"The project has two sides,\" says Xavier Serrat, Neurice project manager and researcher at the University of Barcelona, \"the short-term fight against the snail—and a mid- to long-term fight against climate change. But the snail has given the project greater urgency.\"\nOriginally from South America, the snails were accidentally introduced into the Ebro Delta by Global Aquatic Technologies, a company that raised the snails for fresh-water aquariums(水族馆), but failed to prevent their escape. For now, the giant apple snail's presence in Europe is limited to the Ebro Delta. But the snail continues its march to new territory, says Serrat. \"The question is not whether it will reach other rice-growing areas of Europe, but when.\"\nOver the next year and a half investigators will test the various strains of salt-tolerant rice they've bred. In 2018, farmers will plant the varieties with the most promise in the Ebro Delta and Europe's other two main rice-growing regions—along the Po in Italy, and France's Rhone. A season in the field will help determine which, if any, of the varieties are ready for commercialization.\nAs an EU-funded effort, the search for salt-tolerant varieties of rice is taking place in all three countries. Each team is crossbreeding a local European short-grain rice with a long-grain Asian variety that carries the salt-resistant gene. The scientists are breeding successive generations to arrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance but retain about 97 percent of the European rice genome(基因组).", "48": "The Ebro Delta, in Spain, famous as a battleground during the Spanish Civil War, is now the setting for a different contest, one that is pitting rice farmers against two enemies: the rice-eating giant apple snail, and rising sea levels. What happens here will have a bearing on the future of European rice production and the overall health of southern European wetlands.\nLocated on the Mediterranean just two hours south of Barcelona, the Ebro Delta produces 120 million kilograms of rice a year, making it one of the continent's most important rice-growing areas. As the sea creeps into these fresh-water marshes, however, rising salinity(盐分) is hampering rice production. At the same time, this sea-water also kills off the greedy giant apple snail, an introduced pest that feeds on young rice plants. The most promising strategy has become to harness one foe against the other.\nThe battle is currently being waged on land, in greenhouses at the University of Barcelona. Scientists working under the banner \"Project Neurice\" are seeking varieties of rice that can withstand the increasing salinity without losing the absorbency that makes European rice ideal for traditional Spanish and Italian dishes.\n\"The project has two sides,\" says Xavier Serrat, Neurice project manager and researcher at the University of Barcelona, \"the short-term fight against the snail—and a mid- to long-term fight against climate change. But the snail has given the project greater urgency.\"\nOriginally from South America, the snails were accidentally introduced into the Ebro Delta by Global Aquatic Technologies, a company that raised the snails for fresh-water aquariums(水族馆), but failed to prevent their escape. For now, the giant apple snail's presence in Europe is limited to the Ebro Delta. But the snail continues its march to new territory, says Serrat. \"The question is not whether it will reach other rice-growing areas of Europe, but when.\"\nOver the next year and a half investigators will test the various strains of salt-tolerant rice they've bred. In 2018, farmers will plant the varieties with the most promise in the Ebro Delta and Europe's other two main rice-growing regions—along the Po in Italy, and France's Rhone. A season in the field will help determine which, if any, of the varieties are ready for commercialization.\nAs an EU-funded effort, the search for salt-tolerant varieties of rice is taking place in all three countries. Each team is crossbreeding a local European short-grain rice with a long-grain Asian variety that carries the salt-resistant gene. The scientists are breeding successive generations to arrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance but retain about 97 percent of the European rice genome(基因组).", "49": "The Ebro Delta, in Spain, famous as a battleground during the Spanish Civil War, is now the setting for a different contest, one that is pitting rice farmers against two enemies: the rice-eating giant apple snail, and rising sea levels. What happens here will have a bearing on the future of European rice production and the overall health of southern European wetlands.\nLocated on the Mediterranean just two hours south of Barcelona, the Ebro Delta produces 120 million kilograms of rice a year, making it one of the continent's most important rice-growing areas. As the sea creeps into these fresh-water marshes, however, rising salinity(盐分) is hampering rice production. At the same time, this sea-water also kills off the greedy giant apple snail, an introduced pest that feeds on young rice plants. The most promising strategy has become to harness one foe against the other.\nThe battle is currently being waged on land, in greenhouses at the University of Barcelona. Scientists working under the banner \"Project Neurice\" are seeking varieties of rice that can withstand the increasing salinity without losing the absorbency that makes European rice ideal for traditional Spanish and Italian dishes.\n\"The project has two sides,\" says Xavier Serrat, Neurice project manager and researcher at the University of Barcelona, \"the short-term fight against the snail—and a mid- to long-term fight against climate change. But the snail has given the project greater urgency.\"\nOriginally from South America, the snails were accidentally introduced into the Ebro Delta by Global Aquatic Technologies, a company that raised the snails for fresh-water aquariums(水族馆), but failed to prevent their escape. For now, the giant apple snail's presence in Europe is limited to the Ebro Delta. But the snail continues its march to new territory, says Serrat. \"The question is not whether it will reach other rice-growing areas of Europe, but when.\"\nOver the next year and a half investigators will test the various strains of salt-tolerant rice they've bred. In 2018, farmers will plant the varieties with the most promise in the Ebro Delta and Europe's other two main rice-growing regions—along the Po in Italy, and France's Rhone. A season in the field will help determine which, if any, of the varieties are ready for commercialization.\nAs an EU-funded effort, the search for salt-tolerant varieties of rice is taking place in all three countries. Each team is crossbreeding a local European short-grain rice with a long-grain Asian variety that carries the salt-resistant gene. The scientists are breeding successive generations to arrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance but retain about 97 percent of the European rice genome(基因组).", "50": "The Ebro Delta, in Spain, famous as a battleground during the Spanish Civil War, is now the setting for a different contest, one that is pitting rice farmers against two enemies: the rice-eating giant apple snail, and rising sea levels. What happens here will have a bearing on the future of European rice production and the overall health of southern European wetlands.\nLocated on the Mediterranean just two hours south of Barcelona, the Ebro Delta produces 120 million kilograms of rice a year, making it one of the continent's most important rice-growing areas. As the sea creeps into these fresh-water marshes, however, rising salinity(盐分) is hampering rice production. At the same time, this sea-water also kills off the greedy giant apple snail, an introduced pest that feeds on young rice plants. The most promising strategy has become to harness one foe against the other.\nThe battle is currently being waged on land, in greenhouses at the University of Barcelona. Scientists working under the banner \"Project Neurice\" are seeking varieties of rice that can withstand the increasing salinity without losing the absorbency that makes European rice ideal for traditional Spanish and Italian dishes.\n\"The project has two sides,\" says Xavier Serrat, Neurice project manager and researcher at the University of Barcelona, \"the short-term fight against the snail—and a mid- to long-term fight against climate change. But the snail has given the project greater urgency.\"\nOriginally from South America, the snails were accidentally introduced into the Ebro Delta by Global Aquatic Technologies, a company that raised the snails for fresh-water aquariums(水族馆), but failed to prevent their escape. For now, the giant apple snail's presence in Europe is limited to the Ebro Delta. But the snail continues its march to new territory, says Serrat. \"The question is not whether it will reach other rice-growing areas of Europe, but when.\"\nOver the next year and a half investigators will test the various strains of salt-tolerant rice they've bred. In 2018, farmers will plant the varieties with the most promise in the Ebro Delta and Europe's other two main rice-growing regions—along the Po in Italy, and France's Rhone. A season in the field will help determine which, if any, of the varieties are ready for commercialization.\nAs an EU-funded effort, the search for salt-tolerant varieties of rice is taking place in all three countries. Each team is crossbreeding a local European short-grain rice with a long-grain Asian variety that carries the salt-resistant gene. The scientists are breeding successive generations to arrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance but retain about 97 percent of the European rice genome(基因组).", "51": "Photography was once an expensive, laborious ordeal reserved for life's greatest milestones. Now, the only apparent cost to taking infinite photos of something as common as a meal is the space on your hard drive and your dining companion's patience.\nBut is there another cost, a deeper cost, to documenting a life experience instead of simply enjoying it? \"You hear that you shouldn't take all these photos and interrupt the experience, and it's bad for you, and we're not living in the present moment,\" says Kristin Diehl, associate professor of marketing at the University of Southern California Marshall School of Business.\nDiehl and her fellow researchers wanted to find out if that was true, so they embarked on a series of nine experiments in the lab and in the field testing people's enjoyment in the presence or absence of a camera. The results, published in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, surprised them. Taking photos actually makes people enjoy what they're doing more, not less.\n\"What we find is you actually look at the world slightly differently, because you're looking for things you want to capture, that you may want to hang onto,\" Diehl explains. \"That gets people more engaged in the experience, and they tend to enjoy it more.\"\nTake sightseeing. In one experiment, nearly 200 participants boarded a double-decker bus for a tour of Philadelphia. Both bus tours forbade the use of cell phones but one tour provided digital cameras and encouraged people to take photos. The people who took photos enjoyed the experience significantly more, and said they were more engaged, than those who didn't.\nSnapping a photo directs attention, which heightens the pleasure you get from whatever you're looking at, Diehl says. It works for things as boring as archaeological(考古的) museums, where people were given eye-tracking glasses and instructed either to take photos or not. \"People look longer at things they want to photograph,\" Diehl says. They report liking the exhibits more, too.\nTo the relief of Instagrammers(Instagram用户) everywhere, it can even make meals more enjoyable. When people were encouraged to take at least three photos while they ate lunch, they were more immersed in their meals than those who weren't told to take photos.\nWas it the satisfying click of the camera? The physical act of the snap? No, they found; just the act of planning to take a photo—and not actually taking it—had the same joy-boosting effect. \"If you want to take mental photos, that works the same way,\" Diehl says. \"Thinking about what you would want to photograph also gets you more engaged.\"", "52": "Photography was once an expensive, laborious ordeal reserved for life's greatest milestones. Now, the only apparent cost to taking infinite photos of something as common as a meal is the space on your hard drive and your dining companion's patience.\nBut is there another cost, a deeper cost, to documenting a life experience instead of simply enjoying it? \"You hear that you shouldn't take all these photos and interrupt the experience, and it's bad for you, and we're not living in the present moment,\" says Kristin Diehl, associate professor of marketing at the University of Southern California Marshall School of Business.\nDiehl and her fellow researchers wanted to find out if that was true, so they embarked on a series of nine experiments in the lab and in the field testing people's enjoyment in the presence or absence of a camera. The results, published in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, surprised them. Taking photos actually makes people enjoy what they're doing more, not less.\n\"What we find is you actually look at the world slightly differently, because you're looking for things you want to capture, that you may want to hang onto,\" Diehl explains. \"That gets people more engaged in the experience, and they tend to enjoy it more.\"\nTake sightseeing. In one experiment, nearly 200 participants boarded a double-decker bus for a tour of Philadelphia. Both bus tours forbade the use of cell phones but one tour provided digital cameras and encouraged people to take photos. The people who took photos enjoyed the experience significantly more, and said they were more engaged, than those who didn't.\nSnapping a photo directs attention, which heightens the pleasure you get from whatever you're looking at, Diehl says. It works for things as boring as archaeological(考古的) museums, where people were given eye-tracking glasses and instructed either to take photos or not. \"People look longer at things they want to photograph,\" Diehl says. They report liking the exhibits more, too.\nTo the relief of Instagrammers(Instagram用户) everywhere, it can even make meals more enjoyable. When people were encouraged to take at least three photos while they ate lunch, they were more immersed in their meals than those who weren't told to take photos.\nWas it the satisfying click of the camera? The physical act of the snap? No, they found; just the act of planning to take a photo—and not actually taking it—had the same joy-boosting effect. \"If you want to take mental photos, that works the same way,\" Diehl says. \"Thinking about what you would want to photograph also gets you more engaged.\"", "53": "Photography was once an expensive, laborious ordeal reserved for life's greatest milestones. Now, the only apparent cost to taking infinite photos of something as common as a meal is the space on your hard drive and your dining companion's patience.\nBut is there another cost, a deeper cost, to documenting a life experience instead of simply enjoying it? \"You hear that you shouldn't take all these photos and interrupt the experience, and it's bad for you, and we're not living in the present moment,\" says Kristin Diehl, associate professor of marketing at the University of Southern California Marshall School of Business.\nDiehl and her fellow researchers wanted to find out if that was true, so they embarked on a series of nine experiments in the lab and in the field testing people's enjoyment in the presence or absence of a camera. The results, published in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, surprised them. Taking photos actually makes people enjoy what they're doing more, not less.\n\"What we find is you actually look at the world slightly differently, because you're looking for things you want to capture, that you may want to hang onto,\" Diehl explains. \"That gets people more engaged in the experience, and they tend to enjoy it more.\"\nTake sightseeing. In one experiment, nearly 200 participants boarded a double-decker bus for a tour of Philadelphia. Both bus tours forbade the use of cell phones but one tour provided digital cameras and encouraged people to take photos. The people who took photos enjoyed the experience significantly more, and said they were more engaged, than those who didn't.\nSnapping a photo directs attention, which heightens the pleasure you get from whatever you're looking at, Diehl says. It works for things as boring as archaeological(考古的) museums, where people were given eye-tracking glasses and instructed either to take photos or not. \"People look longer at things they want to photograph,\" Diehl says. They report liking the exhibits more, too.\nTo the relief of Instagrammers(Instagram用户) everywhere, it can even make meals more enjoyable. When people were encouraged to take at least three photos while they ate lunch, they were more immersed in their meals than those who weren't told to take photos.\nWas it the satisfying click of the camera? The physical act of the snap? No, they found; just the act of planning to take a photo—and not actually taking it—had the same joy-boosting effect. \"If you want to take mental photos, that works the same way,\" Diehl says. \"Thinking about what you would want to photograph also gets you more engaged.\"", "54": "Photography was once an expensive, laborious ordeal reserved for life's greatest milestones. Now, the only apparent cost to taking infinite photos of something as common as a meal is the space on your hard drive and your dining companion's patience.\nBut is there another cost, a deeper cost, to documenting a life experience instead of simply enjoying it? \"You hear that you shouldn't take all these photos and interrupt the experience, and it's bad for you, and we're not living in the present moment,\" says Kristin Diehl, associate professor of marketing at the University of Southern California Marshall School of Business.\nDiehl and her fellow researchers wanted to find out if that was true, so they embarked on a series of nine experiments in the lab and in the field testing people's enjoyment in the presence or absence of a camera. The results, published in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, surprised them. Taking photos actually makes people enjoy what they're doing more, not less.\n\"What we find is you actually look at the world slightly differently, because you're looking for things you want to capture, that you may want to hang onto,\" Diehl explains. \"That gets people more engaged in the experience, and they tend to enjoy it more.\"\nTake sightseeing. In one experiment, nearly 200 participants boarded a double-decker bus for a tour of Philadelphia. Both bus tours forbade the use of cell phones but one tour provided digital cameras and encouraged people to take photos. The people who took photos enjoyed the experience significantly more, and said they were more engaged, than those who didn't.\nSnapping a photo directs attention, which heightens the pleasure you get from whatever you're looking at, Diehl says. It works for things as boring as archaeological(考古的) museums, where people were given eye-tracking glasses and instructed either to take photos or not. \"People look longer at things they want to photograph,\" Diehl says. They report liking the exhibits more, too.\nTo the relief of Instagrammers(Instagram用户) everywhere, it can even make meals more enjoyable. When people were encouraged to take at least three photos while they ate lunch, they were more immersed in their meals than those who weren't told to take photos.\nWas it the satisfying click of the camera? The physical act of the snap? No, they found; just the act of planning to take a photo—and not actually taking it—had the same joy-boosting effect. \"If you want to take mental photos, that works the same way,\" Diehl says. \"Thinking about what you would want to photograph also gets you more engaged.\"", "55": "Photography was once an expensive, laborious ordeal reserved for life's greatest milestones. Now, the only apparent cost to taking infinite photos of something as common as a meal is the space on your hard drive and your dining companion's patience.\nBut is there another cost, a deeper cost, to documenting a life experience instead of simply enjoying it? \"You hear that you shouldn't take all these photos and interrupt the experience, and it's bad for you, and we're not living in the present moment,\" says Kristin Diehl, associate professor of marketing at the University of Southern California Marshall School of Business.\nDiehl and her fellow researchers wanted to find out if that was true, so they embarked on a series of nine experiments in the lab and in the field testing people's enjoyment in the presence or absence of a camera. The results, published in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, surprised them. Taking photos actually makes people enjoy what they're doing more, not less.\n\"What we find is you actually look at the world slightly differently, because you're looking for things you want to capture, that you may want to hang onto,\" Diehl explains. \"That gets people more engaged in the experience, and they tend to enjoy it more.\"\nTake sightseeing. In one experiment, nearly 200 participants boarded a double-decker bus for a tour of Philadelphia. Both bus tours forbade the use of cell phones but one tour provided digital cameras and encouraged people to take photos. The people who took photos enjoyed the experience significantly more, and said they were more engaged, than those who didn't.\nSnapping a photo directs attention, which heightens the pleasure you get from whatever you're looking at, Diehl says. It works for things as boring as archaeological(考古的) museums, where people were given eye-tracking glasses and instructed either to take photos or not. \"People look longer at things they want to photograph,\" Diehl says. They report liking the exhibits more, too.\nTo the relief of Instagrammers(Instagram用户) everywhere, it can even make meals more enjoyable. When people were encouraged to take at least three photos while they ate lunch, they were more immersed in their meals than those who weren't told to take photos.\nWas it the satisfying click of the camera? The physical act of the snap? No, they found; just the act of planning to take a photo—and not actually taking it—had the same joy-boosting effect. \"If you want to take mental photos, that works the same way,\" Diehl says. \"Thinking about what you would want to photograph also gets you more engaged.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2018-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about gazpacho?", "options": ["A) It is a typical salad.", "B) It is a Spanish soup.", "C) It is a weird vegetable.", "D) It is a kind of spicy food."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is a Spanish soup.\n【定位】W: Gazpacho is a cold soup from Spain. It's mostly vegetables. I guess you could call it a\nliquid salad.\n女：Gazpacho 是一种来自西班牙的冷汤。它主要由蔬菜做成。我想你可以把它叫作一种液\n体沙拉。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答 + 正面定性\"is a…\"。男方追问\"What's that?\"，女方紧接着正面回答\nGazpacho is a cold soup from Spain.——\"被问者的直接应答\"加上\"is a + 品类\"的定性句\n式，正是答案句信号。\n【替换】a cold soup from Spain ↔ a Spanish soup\nfrom Spain = Spanish，cold soup = soup，同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 a liquid salad，A 抓住 salad 就想让你选；但原话是\"I guess you could call\nit a liquid salad\"，是打比方帮人理解，本质仍是 soup，A 把比喻当定性，是典型原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：I guess you could call it a liquid salad\nC) 原词陷阱\n男方说 Sounds weird.，C 借 weird 这个原词造\"它是种怪蔬菜\"；可 weird 是男方一句感\n叹、且 gazpacho 是汤不是蔬菜，C 既张冠李戴又改了品类。\n借用原文：Cold soup? Sounds weird.\nD) 未提及\n录音从头到尾只说它是冷的、清爽的、主要是蔬菜，从没提\"辣\"。a kind of spicy food 在\n原文找不到任何依据，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "For what purpose is stale bread mixed into gazpacho?", "options": ["A) To make it thicker.", "B) To make it more nutritious.", "C) To add to its appeal.", "D) To replace an ingredient."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To make it thicker.\n【定位】W: No. Bread is crushed and blended in like everything else. It adds texture and thickness to\nthe soup.\n女：不。面包被压碎、像其他食材一样搅打进去。它给汤增加了口感和浓稠度。\n【信号】功能说明句\"It adds … to …\"。It adds texture and thickness to the soup. 用\"It adds X\nto Y\"直接说明加面包带来的作用，是回答\"目的\"的标志性信号句。\n【替换】adds texture and thickness to the soup ↔ To make it thicker\n名词 thickness 转成形容词 thicker，同义\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音谈健康/营养是在后文回答\"is it healthy\"时（说 mostly vegetables），并未把营养归\n为\"加面包\"的目的。B 把别处的\"营养\"话题挪来当本题目的，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：it's mostly vegetables\nC) 未提及\n原文只说加面包增加 texture 和 thickness，没说让它更诱人。To add to its appeal. 是\"加\n东西总能让食物更好吃\"的想当然，原文无据。\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说 Bread is crushed and blended in like everything else——面包是和别的食材一起\n混进去，不是拿来替换谁。To replace an ingredient. 把\"加入\"说成\"替换\"，动作方向反\n了。\n借用原文：Bread is crushed and blended in like everything else", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "Why does the woman think gazpacho is healthy?", "options": ["A) It contains very little fat.", "B) It uses olive oil in cooking.", "C) It uses no artificial additives.", "D) It is mainly made of vegetables."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is mainly made of vegetables.\n【定位】M: Mm. And is it healthy? — W: Sure. As I said earlier, it's mostly vegetables.\n男：嗯。它健康吗？——女：当然。就像我前面说的，它主要是蔬菜。\n【信号】被问\"is it healthy\"后的直接应答 + As I said 回指。男方问 is it healthy?，女方应答\nSure. As I said earlier, it's mostly vegetables.——\"被问者对 healthy 的直接回应\"配\n上\"As I said\"回指强调，正是理由句信号。\n【替换】it's mostly vegetables ↔ It is mainly made of vegetables\nmostly = mainly，同义副词替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音说健康只给了\"蔬菜为主\"一条，从未说脂肪多少。It contains very little fat. 是按\"健康\n=低脂\"的常识脑补，原文无据。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音第6段列食材时念到 olive oil，B 借这个原词造\"用橄榄油所以健康\"；但女方解释健康\n时只字未提油，olive oil 出现在食材介绍处而非理由处，是原词陷阱。\n借用原文：tomato, cucumber, bell peppers, olive oil and stale bread\nC) 未提及\n原文谈健康只说蔬菜为主，没提添加剂的有无。It uses no artificial additives. 同样是健康\n常识的想当然，原文无对应。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say about cured ham?", "options": ["A) It does not go stale for two years.", "B) It takes no special skill to prepare.", "C) It comes from a special kind of pig.", "D) It is a delicacy blended with bread."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It comes from a special kind of pig.\n【定位】W: It's a little dry and salty, and it's very expensive because it comes from a special type of\npig that only eats a special type of food.\n女：它有点干、有点咸，而且非常贵，因为它来自一种只吃特定食物的特殊品种的猪。\n【信号】因果 because 引出独特来源。it comes from a special type of pig 由 because 引出，解\n释它为何昂贵特别——\"因果信号\"指向的这句正面交代来源，是答案句。\n【替换】it comes from a special type of pig ↔ It comes from a special kind of pig\ntype 与 kind 同义，几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 left to hang for up to two years——two years 是腌制悬挂的时长，A 抓住\ntwo years 改成 does not go stale for two years（两年不变质），偷换了这个数字的含\n义，是最毒的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：left to hang for up to two years\nB) 与原文相反\n录音说 cut it themselves into very thin slices with a long flat knife——切薄片用专用长\n刀、显然需要技巧。It takes no special skill to prepare. 把\"需要专门刀工\"说成\"不需技\n巧\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：They cut it themselves into very thin slices with a long flat knife\nD) 张冠李戴\na delicacy blended with bread 里的 bread 来自前文做汤的描述 Bread is crushed and\nblended in，那是 gazpacho 加面包，与火腿无关。D 把汤的做法张冠李戴到火腿头上。\n借用原文：Bread is crushed and blended in like everything else", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman think of table wines?", "options": ["A) They come in a great variety.", "B) They do not make decent gifts.", "C) They do not vary much in price.", "D) They go well with Italian food."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They do not make decent gifts.\n【定位】Our lowest prices are around $6 a bottle, but those are table wines. They are not very special.\nAnd I would not suggest them as a gift.\n我们最低的价位是一瓶6美元左右，但那些是餐酒。它们不怎么特别。我也不建议拿它们当\n礼物。\n【信号】否定评价not suggest…as a gift。答案句的信号是连续的负面评价：They are not very\nspecial. 加上 I would not suggest them as a gift.，两处否定明确表态餐酒不宜送礼，正\n是态度题要抓的评判落点。\n【替换】I would not suggest them as a gift ↔ They do not make decent gifts.\n\"不建议当礼物\"=\"不算像样的礼物\"\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n店员开头说 it varies greatly，指整个酒的价格/种类差异大，并非说\"餐酒\"种类繁多；They\ncome in a great variety. 把这句套到餐酒头上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Well, it varies greatly.\nC) 与原文相反\n录音确实大谈 price，但说的是价格 varies greatly（差异很大），They do not vary much\nin price. 偷了 price/vary 这两个原词，意思却反成\"价差不大\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：Well, it varies greatly.\nD) 未提及\n\"好配食物\"是后文说红酒/意大利酒的优点（见第8段），录音从没说餐酒配意餐；They go\nwell with Italian food. 把别处的优点硬安给餐酒，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What is the price range of wine the man will consider?", "options": ["A) $30-$40.", "B) $40-$50.", "C) $50-$60.", "D) Around $150."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） $30-$40.\n【定位】Mm, yeah. I guess something in the vicinity of 30 or 40 would be good.\n嗯，对。我想30或40块左右的就不错。\n【信号】被问者男士的应答给出自己的价。信号是男士本人的应答：店员报了一堆价后，男士回 I\nguess something in the vicinity of 30 or 40 would be good，这是\"他自己会考虑\"的\n价，正对题干限定的主体。\n【替换】something in the vicinity of 30 or 40 ↔ $30-$40.\nin the vicinity of 30 or 40=$30-$40区间\n【排除】B) 答非所问\n店员建议的体面价位是 between $30 and $60，$40-$50. 落在这个店员区间内却不是男士\n自己说的数；它借\"听过的价位范围\"诱你选，但题干问的是男士考虑的价。\n借用原文：anything between $30 and $60 would make a decent gift\nC) 原词陷阱\n$50-$60. 的 60 来自店员说的 between $30 and $60 的上限，是店员给的范围，并非男士\n亲口考虑的价，盗用了听过的数字。\n借用原文：anything between $30 and $60 would make a decent gift\nD) 原词陷阱\nAround $150. 来自店员说的 our most expensive bottles are over $150，是店里最贵的\n酒，与男士想要中档、不太贵的需求完全相反，是故意念出的离群极值。\n借用原文：our most expensive bottles are over $150", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why does the woman recommend red wines?", "options": ["A) They are a healthy choice for elderly people.", "B) They are especially popular among Italians.", "C) They symbolize good health and longevity.", "D) They go well with different kinds of food."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They go well with different kinds of food.\n【定位】I would say the safest option is always a red wine. They are generally more popular than\nwhites, and can usually be paired with food more easily.\n我会说最稳妥的选择永远是红酒。它们一般比白葡萄酒更受欢迎，而且通常更容易跟食物\n搭配。\n【信号】推荐后紧跟的理由句they can be paired with food。信号是推荐红酒之后立刻给的理\n由：They are generally more popular than whites, and can usually be paired with\nfood more easily.，这正是\"为什么推荐\"的直接解释句。\n【替换】can usually be paired with food more easily ↔ They go well with different kinds of food.\n更易配食物=百搭各种食物\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音说红酒\"受欢迎/好配食物\"，从没提它对老年人健康有益；They are a healthy choice\nfor elderly people. 的\"健康/老人\"录音完全没说，是凭常识脑补。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 more popular 形容红酒整体、Italian 是说本店专长意大利酒，两词都真出现过；\nThey are especially popular among Italians. 把它们拼成\"在意大利人中特别受欢迎\"，录\n音并无此意。\n借用原文：They are generally more popular than whites\nC) 未提及\n录音对红酒的评价止于受欢迎、好配食物，They symbolize good health and longevity.\n把它拔高成\"象征健康长寿\"，是预读时就警觉的煽情拔高，原文未提。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What do we learn about the wine the man finally bought?", "options": ["A) It is a wine imported from California.", "B) It is less spicy than all other red wines.", "C) It is far more expensive than he expected.", "D) It is Italy's most famous type of red wine."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is Italy's most famous type of red wine.\n【定位】This one here is a Chianti, which is perhaps Italy's most famous type of red wine.\n这一瓶是基安蒂，大概是意大利最有名的红酒品种。\n【信号】结尾决定+最高级most famous。两重信号：男士在 I will go with Chianti then. 拍板买\n基安蒂，回扣店员对它的描述 a Chianti, which is perhaps Italy\\'s most famous type of\nred wine，最高级 most famous 是答案落点。\n【替换】perhaps Italy's most famous type of red wine ↔ It is Italy's most famous type of red wine.\n几乎原句对应只去掉perhaps\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音提到 Zinfandel 葡萄 originally native to Croatia but this winery is in eastern\nItaly，根本没有加州；It is a wine imported from California. 既安错了酒（不是他买的\nChianti）又虚构了California，属张冠李戴加臆造。\n借用原文：The grapes are originally native to Croatia but this winery is in eastern Italy\nB) 张冠李戴\na more spicy and peppery flavor 形容的是 Zinfandel，不是男士买的 Chianti；It is less\nspicy than all other red wines. 盗用 spicy 一词且改成\"比所有红酒都不辣\"，对象与极性\n双错。\n借用原文：it has a more spicy and peppery flavor\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说两瓶 Both are similarly priced at just under $40，正在男士预期的30-40档内，毫\n不意外；It is far more expensive than he expected. 说\"远超预期\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：Both are similarly priced at just under $40", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the speaker say many people enjoy doing?", "options": ["A) Learning others' secrets.", "B) Searching for information.", "C) Decoding secret messages.", "D) Spreading sensational news."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Decoding secret messages.\n【定位】Many people enjoy secret codes. The harder the code, the more some people will try to figure\nit out.\n很多人喜欢密码。密码越难，有些人越想把它破解出来。\n【信号】开篇点题句，enjoy 直接对上题干 enjoy doing。录音第一句就是 Many people enjoy\nsecret codes，题干问 enjoy doing，这里 enjoy 原词出现，是直接应答型信号；紧接的\ntry to figure it out 进一步说明他们享受的是把密码破解出来这件事。\n【替换】try to figure it out ↔ Decoding secret messages.\nfigure out a code = decode，同义改写\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nLearning others' secrets. 借了原文 secret codes 里的 secret 制造熟悉感，但原文讲的是\nfigure out 密码本身，不是打探别人的隐私秘密，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：secret codes\nB) 未提及\nSearching for information. 在录音里找不到对应表述，原文只说人们享受破解密码，没有\n谈搜集或查找信息，属无中生有。\nD) 张冠李戴\nSpreading sensational news. 与开篇 enjoy 那句无关；录音后文虽提到 They help the\narmy send news about battles，但那是讲密码在战时的用途、不是人们喜欢做的事，本项\n把 news 张冠李戴还加上原文没有的 sensational。\n借用原文：They help the army send news about battles", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do we learn about the Navajo code talkers?", "options": ["A) They helped the U.S. army in World War Ⅱ.", "B) They could write down spoken codes promptly.", "C) They were assigned to decode enemy messages.", "D) They were good at breaking enemy secret codes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They helped the U.S. army in World War Ⅱ.\n【定位】One very important code was never broken. It was used during World War II by the\nAmericans. And it was developed and used by Navajo Indians.\n有一种非常重要的密码从未被破译。它在二战期间被美国人使用。它是由纳瓦霍印第安人创\n造并使用的。\n【信号】直接陈述句，点出二战、美国、由 Navajo 使用。录音用一组陈述句交代事实：It was\nused during World War II by the Americans 与 it was developed and used by Navajo\nIndians，明确这套密码服务于二战中的美国一方，是描述该群体的关键事实句；第三段\nThey helped troops coordinate their movements and attacks 再次佐证他们帮的是己方\n部队。\n【替换】It was used during World War II by the Americans ↔\nThey helped the U.S. army in World War Ⅱ.\nthe Americans 用此码 = 帮 U.S. army，World War II 原词对上\nThey helped troops coordinate their movements and attacks ↔\nThey helped the U.S. army in World War Ⅱ.\nhelped troops coordinate = helped the U.S. army，详述被概括\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThey could write down spoken codes promptly. 借了原文 It was a spoken code never\nwritten down 里 spoken、written 的字眼，但原文恰恰强调这套密码从不写下来，本项却\n说他们能迅速写下，与原文相反。\n借用原文：It was a spoken code never written down\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey were assigned to decode enemy messages. 堆了 decode、enemy、messages\n一串原文热词制造熟悉感；assigned 也确在原文 One or two would be assigned to a\ngroup of soldiers 出现过。但原文说的是 Navajo 用自己创造的密码发消息帮己方，不是被\n派去破译敌方密码，方向完全相反。\n借用原文：One or two would be assigned to a group of soldiers\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey were good at breaking enemy secret codes. 借了原文反复出现的 code、broken\n（如 One very important code was never broken）；但原文是说他们的密码没人能破，\n不是他们擅长去破敌方密码，方向被偷换。\n借用原文：One very important code was never broken", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What is the speaker mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) Important battles fought in the Pacific War.", "B) Decoding of secret messages in war times.", "C) A military code that was never broken.", "D) Navajo Indians' contribution to code breaking."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） A military code that was never broken.\n【定位】One very important code was never broken. It was used during World War II by the\nAmericans. ... Theirs was the most successful wartime code ever used.\n有一种非常重要的密码从未被破译。它在二战期间被美国人使用。……他们的密码是有史以\n来最成功的战时密码。\n【信号】开头点题 never broken + 结尾收束 most successful 首尾呼应。第二段开篇 One very\nimportant code was never broken 立即点题，第三段结尾 Theirs was the most\nsuccessful wartime code ever used 用最高级收束，首尾都在强调这是一套从未被破、最\n成功的密码，是典型的主旨信号。\n【替换】One very important code was never broken ↔ A military code that was never broken.\nnever broken 原意保留，code 加限定为 military\nTheirs was the most successful wartime code ever used ↔\nA military code that was never broken.\nmost successful wartime code = military code，结尾印证主旨\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nImportant battles fought in the Pacific War. 借了原文 The code talkers worked mostly\nin the islands in the Pacific 与 battles 等词，但原文只把太平洋战役当作密码发挥作用的\n背景，没展开讲具体战役，本项以局部细节冒充主旨。\n借用原文：The code talkers worked mostly in the islands in the Pacific\nB) 过度推断\nDecoding of secret messages in war times. 借了 codes、war 等主题词，但全文重点是\nNavajo 用自家密码而非破译密码，且这一概括过于宽泛，没扣住从未被破这一独特点，属\n以偏概全兼方向偏。\n借用原文：In wartime, codes are especially important\nD) 偷换概念\nNavajo Indians' contribution to code breaking. 用了 Navajo、code 这两个全文核心\n词，极具迷惑性；但原文讲的是 Navajo 创造并使用一套不可破的密码（编码、保密），不\n是他们去破译别人的密码（code breaking），主旨方向被偷换。\n借用原文：The Navajos created the code in their own language", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the speaker say will happen in the next 20 years?", "options": ["A) All services will be personalized.", "B) A lot of knowledge-intensive jobs will be replaced.", "C) Technology will revolutionize all sectors of industry.", "D) More information will be available."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A lot of knowledge-intensive jobs will be replaced.\n【定位】Well, there's going to be a technological replacement of a lot of knowledge-intensive\njobs in the next 20 years, particularly in the two largest sectors of the labor force with\nprofessional skills.\n嗯，在未来 20 年里，会出现对大量知识密集型工作的技术性替代，尤其是在劳动力中两个\n最大的、需要专业技能的领域。\n【信号】时间标志 in the next 20 years 直接对题。这句出现了题干同款时间标志 in the next\n20 years，又紧跟全段开头的 Well 起话，是说话人对\"未来 20 年\"下的核心判断，正是答\n案句。\n【替换】a technological replacement of a lot of knowledge-intensive jobs ↔\nA lot of knowledge-intensive jobs will be replaced.\n名词化转主动+被动改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 All services will be personalized.，蹭了原文 provide information and service 里\n的 service 一词，但原文从没说\"所有服务都将个性化\"，是听到 service 就联想个性化的原\n词陷阱，且 All 范围过绝。\n借用原文：provide information and service\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说 Technology will revolutionize all sectors of industry.，原文只点了 the two largest\nsectors of the labor force\"两个最大领域\"，C 把\"两个\"夸成\"所有行业\"，属范围程度偷\n改。\n借用原文：the two largest sectors of the labor force\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 More information will be available.，蹭了原文 provide information and service\n的 information，但原文是说应用软件\"提供信息和服务\"来替代工作，并非说\"信息会变\n多\"，听到 information 就选即落坑。\n借用原文：provide information and service", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Where will young people have more chances to find jobs?", "options": ["A) In the robotics industry.", "B) In the information service.", "C) In the personal care sector.", "D) In high-end manufacturing."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） In the personal care sector.\n【定位】Now, where will the new jobs be found? Well, the one sector of the economy that can't be\neasily duplicated by even smart technologies is the caring sector—the personal care sector.\n那么，新的工作会在哪里出现呢？嗯，经济中唯一一个连智能技术都不易复制的领域，就是\n看护领域——个人护理领域。\n【信号】设问自答 where…+ the one sector…is。录音先自设问 where will the new jobs be\nfound?，随即用 the one sector…is the personal care sector 自答，设问句后紧跟的答句\n正是答案落点，信号极强。\n【替换】the personal care sector ↔ In the personal care sector.\n几乎原词，仅加介词 In\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 In the robotics industry.，原文确实念到 you can't really get a robot to do a great\nmassage，但 robot 是被当作\"做不了护理\"的反例提的，绝非新工作所在领域，听到 robot\n就选即中招。\n借用原文：you can't really get a robot to do a great massage\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说 In the information service.，蹭的是上一段 provide information and service 的\ninformation service，但那是被技术替代的旧领域，不是新工作领域，正好与题意相反。\n借用原文：provide information and service\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 In high-end manufacturing.，蹭了原文 There could be very high-end personal\nservices. 的 high-end，但原文 high-end 修饰的是\"个人服务\"，根本没提\"制造业\"，是拼\n词造出的陷阱。\n借用原文：There could be very high-end personal services.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the speaker say about therapists?", "options": ["A) They charge high prices.", "B) They need lots of training.", "C) They cater to the needs of young people.", "D) They focus on customers' specific needs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They charge high prices.\n【定位】Therapists do charge a lot of money.\n治疗师确实收费很高。\n【信号】强调助动词 do + 点名 Therapists。这句直接点名 Therapists 并用强调助动词 do（do\ncharge）加重\"确实收费高\"的语气，是说话人对治疗师的明确评述，答案句无疑。\n【替换】Therapists do charge a lot of money ↔ They charge high prices.\na lot of money = high prices 同义替换\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 说 They need lots of training.，原文谈治疗师只说收费高，从未提及他们\"需要大量培\n训\"，是凭\"专业人士想必要培训\"的常识硬加，原文无据。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说 They cater to the needs of young people.，young 蹭自开头 If you are young\nand thinking about your career，但那是对听众说的，与治疗师服务对象无关，听到\nyoung 就联想即落坑。\n借用原文：If you are young and thinking about your career\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 They focus on customers' specific needs.，蹭了原文 the kind of personal\nattention you need 的\"个人需求\"语境，但原文这话是讲护理服务的不可替代性，并非评价\n治疗师\"专注客户具体需求\"，是预读就标记过的\"听着顺\"的分流项。\n借用原文：the kind of personal attention you need", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What is the speaker's book about?", "options": ["A) The rising demand in education and healthcare in the next 20 years.", "B) The disruption caused by technology in traditionally well-paid jobs.", "C) The tremendous changes new technology will bring to people's lives.", "D) The amazing amount of personal attention people would like to have."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） The disruption caused by technology in traditionally well-paid jobs.\n【定位】That's why I wrote this book which is about how to reorganize the economy for the future\nwhen technology brings about destructive changes to what we used to consider high\nincome work.\n这就是我写这本书的原因——这本书讲的是，当技术给我们过去视为高收入的工作带来破坏\n性变化时，如何为未来重新组织经济。\n【信号】结尾收束 That's why…this book is about。末段用 That's why I wrote this book\nwhich is about… 直接收束并点出书的主题，That's why 是结论信号、which is about 直\n接给出书的内容，是主旨答案句。\n【替换】technology brings about destructive changes to what we used to consider high income work ↔\nThe disruption caused by technology in traditionally well-paid jobs.\ndestructive changes=disruption，high income=well-paid\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 说 The rising demand in education and healthcare in the next 20 years.，蹭了第二\n段 teaching and the other, healthcare 和 the next 20 years，但那是中段铺垫的细节，\n且原文说这两领域是\"被替代、岗位流失\"而非\"需求上升\"，既以偏概全又方向反了。\n借用原文：teaching and the other, healthcare\nC) 过度推断\nC 说 The tremendous changes new technology will bring to people's lives.，蹭了\nchanges、technology 等高频词，但\"给人们生活带来的巨变\"比书的实际主题（如何为未\n来重组经济以应对高薪工作受冲击）宽泛太多，是预读标记的范围过宽陷阱。\n借用原文：technology brings about destructive changes\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 The amazing amount of personal attention people would like to have.，几乎照\n搬第三段 there's no limit to the amount of personal attention and personal care\npeople would like，但那只是中段一个分论点，不是书的主题，照搬原句最容易骗到\"听到\n原话就选\"的考生。\n借用原文：there's no limit to the amount of personal attention and personal care people would like", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn from the lecture about the world's oldest paved road in Egypt?", "options": ["A) It was the longest road in ancient Egypt.", "B) It was constructed some 500 years ago.", "C) It lay 8 miles from the monument sites.", "D) It linked a stone pit to some waterways."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It linked a stone pit to some waterways.\n【定位】It linked a stone pit in the Egyptian desert to waterways that carried blocks to monument sites\nalong the Nile.\n它把埃及沙漠里的一处采石场与水路连接起来，这些水路把石块运往尼罗河沿岸的纪念碑\n遗址。\n【信号】道路功能的直接陈述句。紧接在\"发现世界最古老铺装路\"之后，It linked a stone pit in the\nEgyptian desert to waterways 这句直接交代这条路连接了什么，是对道路本身的核心说\n明句，承载着该题答案。\n【替换】It linked a stone pit in the Egyptian desert to waterways ↔\nIt linked a stone pit to some waterways.\n近乎同句压缩，省去修饰\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文只说它是 the only paved road discovered in ancient Egypt（唯一一条），选项偷换\n成 the longest road（最长），借用了 ancient Egypt 这个真词，却把\"唯一\"说成了\"最\n长\"，原文从未提长度对比。\n借用原文：the only paved road discovered in ancient Egypt\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文 at least 500 years older than any previously discovered road 说的是它比以往任何\n路都早至少 500 年，选项却说 constructed some 500 years ago（建于约 500 年前）；\n这条路实为 4600 年历史，500 是\"早出的差值\"，被偷换成了\"建造年代\"。\n借用原文：at least 500 years older than any previously discovered road\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 The 8-mile road 指这条路本身长 8 英里，选项却说 lay 8 miles from the\nmonument sites（离遗址 8 英里）；8 miles 这个真数字被搬去描述\"与遗址的距离\"，原\n文并没说路到遗址有多远。\n借用原文：The 8-mile road", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What did the researchers discover in the stone pit?", "options": ["A) Saws used for cutting stone.", "B) Traces left by early explorers.", "C) An ancient geographical map.", "D) Some stone tool segments."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Saws used for cutting stone.\n【定位】The researchers also made a discovery in the stone pit at the northern end of the road: the first\nevidence that the Egyptians used rock saws. \"This is the oldest example of saws being used\nfor cutting stone,\" said Bown's colleague James Hoffmeier of Wheaton College in Illinois.\n研究者们还在道路北端的采石场里有了一项发现：首次证明埃及人使用过石锯的证据。\"这\n是用锯切石的最早实例，\"鲍恩的同事、伊利诺伊州惠顿学院的詹姆斯·霍夫迈尔说。\n【信号】discovery 信号词 + 冒号引出发现物。made a discovery in the stone pit 正是题干\ndiscover 的对应信号，其后冒号引出的 the first evidence that the Egyptians used rock\nsaws 就是\"发现了什么\"的答案落点。\n【替换】the first evidence that the Egyptians used rock saws ↔ Saws used for cutting stone.\nrock saws 与 saws 同指，补出用途\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文确有 Short segments of the road had been observed by earlier explorers，但说\n的是早期探险者曾观察到道路的零星片段、却没意识到其意义，并非\"在采石场留下痕迹\"；\n选项盗用 explorers 一词另造\"早期探险者的痕迹\"。\n借用原文：Short segments of the road had been observed by earlier explorers\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文说研究者是在 doing geological mapping in the region（做地质测绘）时偶然发现\n这条路，mapping 指测绘活动，不是 An ancient geographical map.（一张古地图）；选\n项把\"测绘\"偷换成\"古地图\"这件实物。\n借用原文：doing geological mapping in the region\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文里 segments 出现在 Short segments of the road（道路的短片段），指被探险者看\n到的道路碎段，而非 Some stone tool segments.（石器碎段）；选项盗用 segments 一\n词，套到\"石器\"上。\n借用原文：Short segments of the road", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "For what purpose was the paved road built?", "options": ["A) To transport stones to block floods.", "B) To provide services for the stone pit.", "C) To link the various monument sites.", "D) To connect the villages along the Nile."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To provide services for the stone pit.\n【定位】The road was clearly built to provide services for the newly discovered stone pit.\n这条路显然是为给新发现的采石场提供服务而修建的。\n【信号】built to 目的状语直接点明。The road was clearly built to provide services for the\nnewly discovered stone pit 用 built to 直接给出修路目的，clearly 又加重了肯定语气，\n是目的题的标准答案句结构。\n【替换】built to provide services for the newly discovered stone pit ↔\nTo provide services for the stone pit.\n近乎原句保留，仅删\"newly discovered\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 floods 出现在讲尼罗河洪水如何抬高湖面、把石块漂运出去的语境（floated over to\nthe Nile during the floods），与\"运石头去挡洪水\"无关；选项 To transport stones to\nblock floods. 借 floods 这个真词编了个原文没有的目的。\n借用原文：floated over to the Nile during the floods\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 carried blocks to monument sites along the Nile 里 monument sites 是石块经水\n路运抵的终点，不是这条路要连接的对象；选项 To link the various monument sites.\n把\"路服务采石场\"偷换成\"路连接各遗址\"，盗用 monument sites 一词。\n借用原文：carried blocks to monument sites along the Nile\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文提到的 the modern villages of el-Lahun and Hawara 只是用来标明河湖相连缺口位\n置的现代村庄，路的目的与连接村庄无关；选项 To connect the villages along the Nile.\n借 villages 一词另造目的。\n借用原文：the modern villages of el-Lahun and Hawara", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker find especially strange?", "options": ["A) Dr. Gong didn't give him any conventional tests.", "B) Dr. Gong marked his office with a hand-painted sign.", "C) Dr. Gong didn't ask him any questions about his pain.", "D) Dr. Gong slipped in needles where he felt no pain."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Dr. Gong slipped in needles where he felt no pain.\n【定位】Other needles were slipped into my left wrist and, strangely, into my right arm, and then into\nboth my closed eyelids.\n还有些针被扎进了我的左手腕，而且奇怪的是，扎进了我的右臂，接着又扎进了我两只闭着\n的眼皮。\n【信号】副词 strangely 直接点破“奇怪”。录音里 strangely 这个副词就是答案信号——题干问什\n么 strange，原文就用 strangely 把要找的点亲口标了出来。它后面紧跟的“扎进右臂、扎进\n闭着的眼皮”这些不疼的部位，正是“怪”在哪里。\n【替换】slipped into my left wrist and, strangely, into my right arm ↔\nslipped in needles where he felt no pain\n具体部位 → 概括为“不疼之处”\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实讲到他做过 two expensive, uncomfortable medical tests，A 借“tests/检查”这\n个真出现过的概念引你上钩，但那是说他在医院做过检查，并非 Dr. Gong“没给他做常规检\n查”，且这一点也不是题干所问的“奇怪之处”。\n借用原文：two expensive, uncomfortable medical tests had failed to produce even a diagnosis\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里办公室确实 marked with a hand-painted sign（手绘招牌），B 一字不差地搬了过\n来，极具迷惑性；但题干问的是“觉得奇怪的事”，手绘招牌只是路上所见的普通细节，作者\n并未称其奇怪，答非所问。\n借用原文：marked with a hand-painted sign\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说医生话不多、Most of my questions to him were greeted with a friendly\nlaugh，但作者强调的是“我向他提的问题多被一笑带过”，并“设法让他知道哪儿疼”，并非医\n生“没问任何关于疼痛的问题”；C 把“医生少说话”偷换成“不问疼痛”，且这也不是被点名的\n“奇怪”之处。\n借用原文：Most of my questions to him were greeted with a friendly laugh, but I managed to let\nhim know where my arm hurt", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "Why did the speaker go see Dr. Gong?", "options": ["A) He had heard of the wonders acupuncture could work.", "B) Dr. Gong was very famous in New York's Chinatown.", "C) Previous medical treatments failed to relieve his pain.", "D) He found the expensive medical tests unaffordable."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Previous medical treatments failed to relieve his pain.\n【定位】Several trips to a hospital and two expensive, uncomfortable medical tests had failed to\nproduce even a diagnosis.\n几次去医院、两次又贵又难受的医学检查，连个诊断结果都没能给出。\n【信号】failed 表努力落空，引出转投。录音里 had failed to produce even a diagnosis 的 failed\n是答案信号——它点明此前西医的种种努力全部落空，正是“于是决定改试针灸、来找 Dr.\nGong”的直接原因。这种“前面白费”紧接“另寻出路”是叙事里典型的因果落点。\n【替换】medical tests had failed to produce even a diagnosis ↔\nPrevious medical treatments failed to relieve his pain\n“查不出病因”=“先前治疗没缓解疼痛”\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音里他是经 A Chinese-American friend recommended Dr. Gong（朋友推荐）才去\n的，从没说“听说针灸有多神奇”。A 是合理却未提及的动机，属凭常识脑补。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音只说 Dr. Gong 的办公室在 New York's Chinatown（唐人街），B 借“Chinatown”这\n个真念过的地名，编出“他在唐人街很有名”这个原文未提的判断，且“有名”也不是他去看病\n的原因。\n借用原文：the office of Dr. James Gong in New York's Chinatown\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说检查 two expensive, uncomfortable medical tests（又贵又难受），D 抓住\nexpensive 引诱，但作者只说贵和难受、且查不出病，从未说“贵到付不起\n（unaffordable）”而放弃。把“贵”偷换成“负担不起”，程度被改了。\n借用原文：two expensive, uncomfortable medical tests had failed to produce even a diagnosis", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What accounts for the growing popularity of acupuncture in the United States, according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) More and more patients ask for the treatment.", "B) Acupuncture techniques have been perfected.", "C) It doesn't need the conventional medical tests.", "D) It does not have any negative side effects."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） More and more patients ask for the treatment.\n【定位】Nowadays, a lot of medical doctors have learned acupuncture techniques; so have a number\nof dentists. Reason? Patient demand.\n如今很多西医也学会了针灸技术，不少牙医也学了。原因？病人的需求。\n【信号】自问自答 Reason? 直接点名原因。录音用 Reason? 一个自问、紧接 Patient demand. 一\n个自答，把原因亲口点了出来——这种“设问—解答”结构是答案信号，作者等于替你画了重\n点。听到 Reason? 就守住它后面那两个词。\n【替换】Patient demand ↔ More and more patients ask for the treatment\n名词 demand → 展开为“病人要求”\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音明说 nobody really knows what's happening（没人真弄得清针灸怎么起效），可见\n技术远谈不上完善；B 的 perfected（已臻完美）与原文意思相反，且 techniques 一词是\n盗用原文 acupuncture techniques 来增加迷惑。\n借用原文：though acupuncture is at least 2,200 years old, nobody really knows what's happening\nC) 答非所问\n“不做常规检查”是 Dr. Gong 看病时的做法（见第三段他没做检查就动手），录音从未把它\n列为针灸“在全美走红”的原因。C 把前文一个细节安到“流行原因”这个题上，答非所问。\nD) 未提及\n录音通篇没讲针灸有没有副作用，更没把“无副作用”说成流行的原因。D 是听上去合理、实\n则原文没提的项，靠常识脑补就会中招。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say about Ronald and Lois's early years of married life?", "options": ["A) They were on the verge of breaking up.", "B) They were compatible despite differences.", "C) They quarreled a lot and never resolved their arguments.", "D) They argued persistently about whether to have children."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They quarreled a lot and never resolved their arguments.\n【定位】But in the early years of their marriage, both were disturbed by persistent arguments that\nseem to fade away without ever being truly resolved.\n但在婚姻最初的几年里，两人都被持续不断的争吵所困扰，这些争吵似乎只是渐渐平息，却\n从未真正得到解决。\n【信号】转折 But 后才是真意。前一句说他们自认是幸福夫妻，紧接 But in the early years of\ntheir marriage 一转，转折后这句才是早年真相；转折词 But 就是答案句信号，前半的幸福\n是干扰。\n【替换】persistent arguments ↔ quarreled a lot\n持续争吵=吵得多\nwithout ever being truly resolved ↔ never resolved their arguments\n从未解决的肯否定对等\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n录音只说被争吵困扰，从未提到濒临分手；on the verge of breaking up 把吵架夸大成要\n分手，是过度推断，原文还说他们如今 happy couple，恰恰没分。\nB) 原词陷阱\ncompatible despite differences 说有差异但合得来，是个正向判断，而录音早年讲的是被\n争吵困扰、未解决，方向相反；compatible 一词倒是录音后文 how compatible you are\nwith your mate 出现过，借来骗听到原词就想选。\n借用原文：how compatible you are with your mate\nD) 未提及\n录音说争吵 persistent 却没说吵的是什么内容，更没提孩子；about whether to have\nchildren 给争吵硬安了要不要孩子这个话题，原文未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn about Ronald and Lois?", "options": ["A) Neither of them has any brothers or sisters.", "B) Neither of them won their parents' favor.", "C) They weren't spoiled in their childhood.", "D) They didn't like to be the apple of their parents' eyes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Neither of them has any brothers or sisters.\n【定位】Ronald and Lois are only children, and \"onlies\" grow up accustomed to being the apple of\ntheir parents' eyes.\n罗纳德和罗伊斯都是独生子女，而独生子女从小习惯于做父母的掌上明珠。\n【信号】人名后的身份定性句。第 2 段开头直接 Ronald and Lois are only children，紧跟人名给出\n身份，这种主语就是题中人物、谓语 are 给定性的句子就是答案句。\n【替换】Ronald and Lois are only children ↔ Neither of them has any brothers or sisters\n独生=都没有兄弟姐妹\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音说独生子女 being the apple of their parents' eyes（是父母掌上明珠），即极受偏\n爱，而 Neither of them won their parents' favor 说两人都没得到父母偏爱，与录音正好\n相反，却借了 parents 这一原词来迷惑。\n借用原文：being the apple of their parents' eyes\nC) 与原文相反\n录音讲独生子女习惯做掌上明珠，意味着是被娇宠的；They weren't spoiled in their\nchildhood 说童年没被宠坏，与 the apple of parents' eyes 透出的受宠相反，属反向干\n扰。\n借用原文：accustomed to being the apple of their parents' eyes\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey didn't like to be the apple of their parents' eyes 原封盗用录音的 the apple of\ntheir parents' eyes，但录音说他们习惯且乐于做掌上明珠，并未说他们不喜欢，didn't\nlike 是凭空加的否定，答非所问。\n借用原文：the apple of their parents' eyes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the oldest child in a family?", "options": ["A) They are usually good at making friends.", "B) They tend to be adventurous and creative.", "C) They are often content with what they have.", "D) They tend to be self-assured and responsible."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They tend to be self-assured and responsible.\n【定位】The oldest tends to be self-assured, responsible, a high achiever and relatively serious\nand reserved.\n老大往往自信、有责任感、成就高，并且相对严肃、内敛。\n【信号】分类句 tends to be 后的形容词串。第 4 段以 The oldest tends to be 开头给老大贴标\n签，tends to be 后那串形容词就是该类人的性格定性，是答案句信号。\n【替换】self-assured, responsible ↔ self-assured and responsible\n形容词近乎原词对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说老大 He may be slow to make friends（交友慢），good at making friends 却说\n善于交友，恰好相反，却借了 make friends 这一原词诱选。\n借用原文：He may be slow to make friends\nB) 张冠李戴\nadventurous and creative 是录音里描述老幺的词 outgoing, adventurous, optimistic,\ncreative，并非老大；把讲老幺的形容词安到老大头上，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：outgoing, adventurous, optimistic, creative\nC) 原词陷阱\ncontent with what they have 看似与录音 content with only one companion 沾边，但\n录音 content 说的是老大满足于只有一个朋友，并非满足于现状；选项盗用 content 偷换\n了宾语。\n借用原文：content with only one companion", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the only children?", "options": ["A) They enjoy making friends.", "B) They tend to be well adjusted.", "C) They are least likely to take initiative.", "D) They usually have successful marriages."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They tend to be well adjusted.\n【定位】But since an \"only,\" tends to be a well-adjusted individual, she'll eventually learn to relate to\nany chosen spouse.\n但由于独生子女往往是个适应良好的人，她最终会学会与所选择的任何配偶相处。\n【信号】tends to be 给出的定性。第 7 段 tends to be a well-adjusted individual 用 tends to\nbe 直接给独生子女定性，这就是该类人的核心特征句，是答案句信号。\n【替换】tends to be a well-adjusted individual ↔ tend to be well adjusted\n近乎原词的定性对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说独生子女 most comfortable when alone（独处最自在），enjoy making friends\n说爱交友，与独处倾向相反；making friends 是录音讲老大时的字眼，被挪来诱选。\n借用原文：most comfortable when alone\nC) 张冠李戴\nleast likely to take initiative 几乎原句搬自讲中间孩子那段的 less likely to take\ninitiative，说的是中间子女不是独生子女；把中间孩子的特征安到独生头上，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：less likely to take initiative\nD) 过度推断\n录音只说独生子女最终能学会与任意配偶相处，并未下婚姻成功的结论；successful\nmarriages 把会相处夸成婚姻美满，原文未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) puzzled\n动词\n【词性槽】have ___ 现在完成时谓语，要过去分词\n【依据】“多个世纪以来一直使专家困惑”——puzzle sb. 标准搭配\n上下文 as the \"more than 21 acres of hard, white casing stones\" that originally\ncovered it were __(27)__ long ago 解释了为什么尺寸难测——外层石材早就没了，所以专\n家长期搞不清精确尺寸；puzzle sb.（使某人困惑）正是这种长期未解的语义。\n【竞争词】C) established “确立专家”宾语不对\nestablish 的宾语通常是“制度、地位、事实”而非“专家”，has established experts 在搭配上讲\n不通。\nM) removed “移走专家”语义荒谬\nremove experts 字面是“移走专家”，与上下文（尺寸难定）毫无关联；且 M 真正用在 27 题“石\n材被移走”的语境里。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) removed\n动词\n【词性槽】were ___ long ago 被动语态，要过去分词\n【依据】casing 石早就被“拆走”了，正解释“尺寸难测”\n本句解释为何 26 题中“尺寸困扰专家”——因为 the \"more than 21 acres of hard, white\ncasing stones\" that originally covered it were __(27)__ long ago，外层石材早已被移\n走，团队才不得不去 finding any surviving __(29)__ of the casing；remove（移走、拆\n除）与“surviving remnants（残存碎片）”形成自然因果。\n【竞争词】C) established “石材被确立”不通\nestablish stones 不是合理搭配，且即便译成“被建立”，也无法解释下文为何只能找到残存碎\n片；语义因果链断裂。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) chronicles\n动词\n【词性槽】which ___ the work … 关系从句缺谓语，主语单数\n【依据】“记录/编录”研究协会的工作——与 newsletter 职能匹配\nReporting in the most recent issue of the newsletter \"AERAGRAM,\" which __(28)__\nthe work of the Ancient Egypt Research Associates 中，newsletter（简报）作为定期出\n版物，最自然的职能就是按时间“记录、记述”研究协会的工作；chronicle 作动词正是“按时\n间顺序记述”，与 newsletter 的定位严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】D) fascinates “简报使工作着迷”主宾颠倒\nfascinate 意为“使……着迷”，其宾语应是“人”而非“工作”；the newsletter fascinates the work\n主宾不通；且 D 真正用在 31 题“最令他着迷的问题”的语境里。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) remnants\n名词\n【词性槽】any 后＋of 短语限定，定语法槽为可数名词复数\n【依据】casing 已被拆除，能找到的只剩“残余物”\n上下文 the \"more than 21 acres of hard, white casing stones\" that originally covered\nit were __(27)__ long ago 表明外层石灰岩早已被拆走，团队只能去找它“surviving 残存\n的”部分；remnants（残余物）正好与 surviving 同义呼应，复数也匹配 any 后可数名词的\n形态。\n【竞争词】E) hypothesis “假说”不会“surviving of the casing”\nhypothesis 指抽象的“假说”，无法和 of the casing（外层包石的）搭配，本空讲的是物理遗存\n而非观点。\nG) momentum “动量/势头”与考古实物语境无关\nmomentum 多用于物理或抽象“势头”，谈不上 surviving 或属于 casing 的一部分。\nJ) precision “精度”是抽象属性，不会被“surviving”修饰\nprecision 是不可数抽象名词，且 any surviving precision of the casing 在语义上不通——精\n度不会“幸存”。\nN) revelations “被披露之事”与外层石头物理遗存对不上\nrevelations 指被揭示出来的事实或秘密，属于信息层面，不能用来指物质上仍幸存的外层石材\n片段。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) maximum\n名词\n【词性槽】a ___ of 5.5 inches 名词＋of＋数值，空处为名词\n【依据】“最多短 5.5 英寸”——a maximum of 标准量词搭配\na maximum of + 数值 = “最多……”，与 only by much 的语义（差距不大、最多 5.5 英\n寸）一致；同时对应开篇 But really not by much. 的总评——差距很小、且是最大值不超\n过 5.5 英寸。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) fascinates\n动词\n【词性槽】that 引导的定语从句缺谓语，主语 that 指代单数 question\n【依据】“最令他着迷的问题”——fascinate sb. 标准搭配\n下文 isn't how the Egyptians who designed and built the pyramid got it wrong 4,500\nyears ago, but how they got it so close to __(32)__ 描述 Dash 真正感兴趣的不是“他们\n怎么错的”而是“他们怎么做得这么准”，正是“最令他着迷的问题”；fascinate sb.（使某人着\n迷）与 the question 主语搭配自然。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) perfect\n形容词\n【词性槽】be close to ___ 中 to 作介词，空处可为名词或形容词；并列结构定形容词\n【依据】close to perfect＝接近完美，正面照应主旨\n全文主旨即“误差极小、几乎完美”——首段 the Great Pyramid of Giza is not exactly\neven. But really not by much. 与 33 题 with such precision、35 题 only slightly away\n三处呼应，全部指向“perfect”而非“complete”；close to perfect 也是英文里描述“近乎完\n美”的标准搭配。\n【竞争词】B) complete “完整”谈的是有没有缺件，与“准不准”错位\ncomplete 强调“完整、完成”，是“缺不缺零件”的维度；本句的对照是 wrong（错的）↔\nright/perfect（对的、完美的），是“准不准、对不对齐”的维度，二者不在一条轴上。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— complete 看似“完整”很对，实为 wrong ↔ ___ 对照轴错\n位。got it wrong 4,500 years ago, but how they got it so close to __(32)__ 中 wrong\n是“做错了”，对照的应是“做对了/接近完美”，所以选 perfect 而非 complete；防坑：见到\nnot … wrong, but … ___，立刻在词库里找 wrong 的反义形容词。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) precision\n名词\n【词性槽】with such ___ 的固定结构里要名词\n【依据】全段在赞叹“精度”——precision 直对主题\n下文 its north-south axis runs 3 minutes 54 seconds west of due north 用极细的角度\n数据具体说明误差极小，与 with such precision（如此精准）正好对应；上文 how they\ngot it so close to __(32)__ 也已铺垫“接近完美”的语义。\n【竞争词】E) hypothesis “以如此假说画线”不通\nwith such hypothesis 字面意为“以如此假说”，与 lay out lines（放样画线）这一物理动作搭不\n上；且 hypothesis 后面紧跟 his __(34)__ 处才是它真正的归宿。\nG) momentum “动量/势头”与放样动作不搭\nwith momentum 多用于运动、推进语境，与“放样画对齐线”的精细工艺无关；语义指向是“速\n度/势能”，不是“准确度”。\nN) revelations 复数 + 抽象“启示”与 with such 不配\nrevelations 是复数可数名词，with such revelations 也讲不通——古埃及人画线不是靠“启示”\n而是靠工具与方法。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) hypothesis\n名词\n【词性槽】his ___ is that … 名词从句作表语，空处必为名词\n【依据】“假说”由 that 从句解释其具体内容\nWe can only speculate as to how the Egyptians could have laid out these lines 已用\nspeculate（推测）定调；Dash 提出的“他们用网格放样”只是推测、未经证实，正是\nhypothesis（假说）。his hypothesis is that … = “他的假说是……”句式典型。\n【竞争词】G) momentum “他的动量”和 is that 从句对不上\nmomentum 不接“that 内容从句”，his momentum is that … 不成立，且与“推测画线方式”的\n话题无关。\nN) revelations 复数且义为“被揭示之事”，与未经证实的推测相反\nrevelations 暗示已经被披露的事实，而本句明说 We can only speculate（只能推测），与“揭\n示出来的真相”语义相反；况且复数与 is 单数也不一致。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— revelations 看似与“想法/发现”相关，实为与 speculate 反\n义。his __(34)__ is that the Egyptians laid out their design on a grid 中，his + is that\n句式要求观点类抽象名词，但上句 We can only speculate 已定调“只能推测”，必填\nhypothesis（未证实的假说）而非 revelations（被披露的事实）；防坑：上文 speculate /\nguess 等动词出现，就把 revelations / truth / discovery 这类“已确证”名词排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) chronicles", "B) complete", "C) established", "D) fascinates", "E) hypothesis", "F) maximum", "G) momentum", "H) mysteriously", "I) perfect", "J) precision", "K) puzzled", "L) remnants", "M) removed", "N) revelations", "O) slightly"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) slightly\n副词\n【词性槽】is oriented only ___ away from … 副词修饰整个介短\n【依据】下文角度差极小——slightly 量上吻合\n括号里 its north-south axis runs 3 minutes 54 seconds west of due north, while its\neast-west axis runs 3 minutes 51 seconds north of due east 给出的偏差只有几分秒，\narcheologist 也评价 an amount that's \"tiny, but similar,\"——只能说 slightly（略微地）\n偏离正北/正东，正面回应 32 题“接近完美”的总评。\n【竞争词】H) mysteriously “神秘地偏离”与作者赞叹精度的态度相悖\nmysteriously 表“神秘地”，会暗示原因不明、带玄学色彩；而本段重点是用精确数据印证“几乎\n完美对齐”，态度是赞叹精度而非渲染神秘。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is thought probable that the human brain is particularly good at picking up socially important information.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】核心命题——人脑擅长接收具有社会重要性的信息\n【定位】Matthew D. Lieberman, a social cognitive neuroscientist at the University of California, Los\nAngeles, and author of the 2013 book Social: Why Our Brains Are Wired to Connect,\nsuspects that the human brain is especially skillful at learning socially significant information.\n加州大学洛杉矶分校的社会认知神经科学家、2013 年《社交：为何我们的大脑天生需要连\n接》一书作者 Matthew D. Lieberman 怀疑，人脑特别擅长学习具有社会意义的信息。\n【改写】It is thought probable that the human brain is particularly good at picking up socially important\ninformation\n↔\nsuspects that the human brain is especially skillful at learning socially significant information\n同义副词 + 同义形容词 + 同义动词短语 + 同义宾语逐一替换\n【辨邻】 I I 段聚焦于 this network can be called on to process and store the kind of\ninformation taught in school 这一应用层主张，并未陈述「人脑特别擅长学习社会重\n要信息」这一上位命题，命题层次不同。\nthis network can be called on to process and store the kind of information taught in school—\npotentially giving students access to a range of untapped mental powers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "It can be concluded from experiments that the presence of peers increases risk-taking by adolescents and youth.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】实验结论——同伴在场增加青少年与年轻人冒险\n【定位】\"The presence of peers makes adolescents and youth, but not adults, more likely to take\nrisks,\" Steinberg and Gardner concluded.\nSteinberg 与 Gardner 总结道：「同伴在场让青少年和年轻人——但不让成年人——更可\n能去冒险。」\n【改写】the presence of peers increases risk-taking by adolescents and youth ↔\nThe presence of peers makes adolescents and youth, but not adults, more likely to take risks\n主语原话保留 + 谓语动词同义 + 宾语名词化\n【辨邻】 F F 段聚焦 greater activity in two regions associated with rewards 与 rewards are\nmore intense for teens when they are with peers，讲的是奖赏感强化，并未陈述\n「同伴在场让青少年和年轻人更愿冒险」这一驾驶实验结论。\nrewards are more intense for teens when they are with peers, which motivates them to pursue\nhigher-risk experiences", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Students should be told that risk-taking in the classroom can be something positive.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】应告诉学生课堂中的冒险是积极的\n【定位】We should assure such students that risk, and even peer pressure, can be a good thing—as\nlong as it happens in the classroom and not in the car.\n我们应该让这些学生确信：冒险——甚至同伴压力——可以是好事，只要它发生在教室里而\n不是车里。\n【改写】Students should be told that risk-taking in the classroom can be something positive ↔\nWe should assure such students that risk, and even peer pressure, can be a good thing—as\nlong as it happens in the classroom and not in the car\n建议主体同义 + 正面评价同义 + 教室情境同义\n【辨邻】 G G 段聚焦 teenagers learn more quickly and more effectively when their peers\nare present，是关于学习效率的实验结论；虽然结尾提到 this finding could have\nimportant implications for how we think about educating adolescents，但并未\n提出「应告诉学生冒险是积极的」这一具体建议。\nthis finding could have important implications for how we think about educating adolescents", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The urge of finding a mate and getting married accounts for adolescents' greater attention to social interactions.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】找伴侣与结婚的冲动解释青少年更关注社交\n【定位】Evolutionarily speaking, people in this age group are at a stage in which they can prepare\nto find a mate and start their own family while separating from parents and striking out on\ntheir own.\n从进化角度讲，这一年龄段的人正处在一个阶段：他们可以准备寻找伴侣、组建自己的家\n庭，同时脱离父母、独立生活。\n【改写】The urge of finding a mate and getting married ↔\nthey can prepare to find a mate and start their own family\nfind a mate 原样复现 + getting married 概括 start their own family\naccounts for adolescents' greater attention to social interactions ↔\ntheir brain prompts them to think and even obsess about others\n因果关系的同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "According to Steinberg, the presence of peers increases the speed and effectiveness of teenagers' learning.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Steinberg 所说——同伴在场提升青少年学习的速度与效率\n【定位】\"What our study suggests is that teenagers learn more quickly and more effectively when\ntheir peers are present than when they're on their own,\" Steinberg says.\nSteinberg 说：「我们的研究表明，青少年在同伴在场时比独自一人时学得更快、更\n有效。」\n【改写】the presence of peers increases the speed and effectiveness of teenagers' learning ↔\nteenagers learn more quickly and more effectively when their peers are present\n条件状语转主语 + 副词转名词\n【辨邻】 F F 段聚焦 rewards are more intense for teens when they are with peers, which\nmotivates them to pursue higher-risk experiences，讲的是动机层面而不是学习\n效率。\nrewards are more intense for teens when they are with peers, which motivates them to pursue\nhigher-risk experiences that might bring a big payoff", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Teenagers' parents are often concerned about negative peer influence.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】家长担忧负面同伴影响\n【定位】Parents of teenagers often view their children's friends with something like suspicion. They\nworry that the adolescent peer group has the power to push its members into behavior that is\nfoolish and even dangerous.\n青少年的父母常常以类似怀疑的眼光看待孩子的朋友。他们担心青少年同伴群体有力量把成\n员推向愚蠢甚至危险的行为。\n【改写】Teenagers' parents are often concerned ↔\nParents of teenagers often view their children's friends with something like suspicion\nconcerned 与 view ... with suspicion 同义\nnegative peer influence ↔\nthe adolescent peer group has the power to push its members into behavior that is foolish and\neven dangerous\n概括上位——negative 概括 foolish and dangerous\n【辨邻】 D D 段聚焦 a crowd's influence need not always be negative——讨论者是研究者，\n目的是反思「是否总是负面」，并非陈述「父母担忧负面同伴影响」。\nthey came to suspect that a crowd's influence need not always be negative", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Activating the brain's social network involved in socially motivated learning and memory may allow students to tap unused mental powers.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】激活与社会动机学习相关的大脑社会网络可调用未开发的心智能力\n【定位】Such findings, he says, suggest that \"this network can be called on to process and store the\nkind of information taught in school—potentially giving students access to a range of\nuntapped mental powers.\"\nLieberman 说，这些发现表明：「这一网络可被调来处理与存储学校所教的那类信息，从而\n有可能让学生用上一系列未被开发的心智能力。」\n【改写】the brain's social network ↔ part of the brain's social network\n原话保留\nsocially motivated learning and memory ↔\na distinct network involved in socially motivated learning and memory\n原话保留\nallow students to tap unused mental powers ↔\npotentially giving students access to a range of untapped mental powers\n动词同义 + 形容词转动词搭配\n【辨邻】 H H 段聚焦 the human brain is especially skillful at learning socially significant\ninformation 这一总命题，未提 untapped mental powers 这一应用层主张。\npeople could better remember these statements when given a social motivation", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The presence of peers intensifies the feeling of rewards in teens' brains.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】同伴在场强化青少年大脑里的奖赏感\n【定位】The brains of teenagers, but not adults, showed greater activity in two regions associated with\nrewards when they were being observed by same-age peers than when alone. In other words,\nrewards are more intense for teens when they are with peers, which motivates them to pursue\nhigher-risk experiences that might bring a big payoff.\n在被同龄同伴注视时，青少年（而非成年人）的大脑在两个与奖赏相关的区域显示出更强的\n活动。换句话说，同伴在场时奖赏感对青少年来说更强烈，这促使他们去追求可能带来重大\n回报的更高风险体验。\n【改写】The presence of peers intensifies the feeling of rewards in teens' brains ↔\nrewards are more intense for teens when they are with peers\n条件状语与主语互换 + 动词与形容词比较级互换\n【辨邻】 C C 段聚焦 50 percent higher on an index of risky driving，是行为表现层面；F 段才\n陈述 rewards are more intense for teens when they are with peers 这一脑机制层\n面命题。\nOlder adolescents scored about 50 percent higher on an index of risky driving when their peers\nwere in the room", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "When we absorb information for the purpose of imparting it to others, we do so with greater accuracy and depth.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为教别人而吸收信息会更准更深\n【定位】Research shows that when we absorb information in order to teach it to someone else,\nwe learn it more accurately and deeply, perhaps in part because we are engaging our\nsocial cognition.\n研究表明，当我们为教别人而吸收信息时，我们学得更准确、更深入，这或许部分是因为我\n们调动了自己的社会认知。\n【改写】absorb information for the purpose of imparting it to others ↔\nabsorb information in order to teach it to someone else\n目的状语同义 + 动词同义\nwe do so with greater accuracy and depth ↔ we learn it more accurately and deeply\n副词转名词化短语\n【辨邻】 G G 段聚焦 teenagers learn more quickly and more effectively when their peers\nare present，条件是「同伴在场」；K 段才陈述「为教别人而学习」这一条件。\nteenagers learn more quickly and more effectively when their peers are present than when they're\non their own", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some experts are suggesting that we turn peer influence to good use in education.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】专家建议把同伴影响为教育所用\n【定位】Now some experts are proposing that we should take advantage of the teen brain's keen\nsensitivity to the presence of friends and leverage it to improve education.\n现在一些专家提议，我们应该利用青少年大脑对朋友在场的高度敏感性，并将其用于改进\n教育。\n【改写】Some experts are suggesting ↔ some experts are proposing\n动词同义\nturn peer influence to good use in education ↔\ntake advantage of the teen brain's keen sensitivity to the presence of friends and leverage it to\nimprove education\n动词短语同义 + 抽象命题等价\n【辨邻】 K K 段聚焦 In Social, Lieberman lays out a number of ways to do so，讲的是具体怎\n么做（如把拿破仑、丘吉尔放到心理动机视角下讲）；D 段才是「一些专家提议要利用\n同伴影响改进教育」这一上位命题的源头。\nIn Social, Lieberman lays out a number of ways to do so", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why does the author mention the Spanish Civil War at the beginning of the passage?", "options": ["A) It had great impact on the life of Spanish rice farmers.", "B) It is of great significance in the records of Spanish history.", "C) Rice farmers in the Ebro Delta are waging a battle of similar importance.", "D) Rice farmers in the Ebro Delta are experiencing as hard a time as in the war."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （例证目的题）\n【判型】Why does the author mention the Spanish Civil War 直接问作者为什么提到某事，属于\n典型的写作意图/例证作用类提问。\n【拆句】题干主干是 Why does the author mention the Spanish Civil War，由 at the beginning\nof the passage 这一介词短语作状语补充位置；句式平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The Ebro Delta, in Spain, famous as a battleground during the Spanish Civil War, is now the\nsetting for a different contest, one that is pitting rice farmers against two enemies: the rice-\neating giant apple snail, and rising sea levels.\n西班牙的 Ebro 三角洲，曾因是西班牙内战的战场而出名，如今则成为另一场\"战斗\"的舞台\n——一场让稻农同时对抗两大敌人（食稻的巨型苹果螺与海平面上升）的对决。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt had great impact on the life of Spanish rice farmers 把\"内战影响稻农生活\"当作作\n者提及内战的目的，但原文并未谈论内战对稻农生活的任何影响。\nB) 答非所问\nIt is of great significance in the records of Spanish history 只解释\"内战在西班牙史\n上很重要\"，没有回答\"作者为何在此处提它\"——这是常识，而题问的是写作意图。\nC) ✓ 正确\nis now the setting for a different contest, one that is pitting rice farmers against two enemies ↔\nRice farmers in the Ebro Delta are waging a battle of similar importance\nWhat happens here will have a bearing on the future of European rice production and the overall\nhealth of southern European wetlands\n↔ a battle of similar importance\n预判为\"用历史战场衬托今日之战同样重要\"，C 项的 a battle of similar importance 同\n时承接 a different contest 与 will have a bearing on the future of European rice\nproduction，与预判一致。\nD) 过度推断\nRice farmers in the Ebro Delta are experiencing as hard a time as in the war 把作\n者的比喻坐实为\"稻农像战时一样艰难\"，原文只做\"战场\"意象的类比，并未比较两个时代\n生活艰苦程度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What may be the most effective strategy for rice farmers to employ in fighting their enemies?", "options": ["A) Striking the weaker enemy first.", "B) Killing two birds with one stone.", "C) Eliminating the enemy one by one.", "D) Using one evil to combat the other."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What may be the most effective strategy 问具体做法，可在原文找到一句直接对应的策\n略陈述。\n【拆句】题干主干 What may be the most effective strategy，后接不定式 to employ in fighting\ntheir enemies 作 strategy 的后置修饰，意为\"在对抗敌人时所采用的最有效策略\"；情态动\n词 may 表推测，并不改变设问性质。\n【定位】At the same time, this sea-water also kills off the greedy giant apple snail, an introduced pest\nthat feeds on young rice plants. The most promising strategy has become to harness one foe\nagainst the other.\n与此同时，这种海水也会杀死贪婪的巨型苹果螺——一种以幼稻为食的外来害虫。最有希望\n的策略已经变成\"用一个敌人去对付另一个敌人\"。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nStriking the weaker enemy first 凭空增加\"先打较弱的那个\"，原文从未对两敌人\n作\"强/弱\"区分，也未说明先后。\nB) 偷换概念\nKilling two birds with one stone 是\"用一种手段达成两个目标\"，与原文\"让两个敌人\n相互制约\"不是同一回事；这里没有\"一个手段\"统揽两敌，而是\"一个敌人本身\"被借用为\n手段。\nC) 与原文相反\nEliminating the enemy one by one 是\"逐一消灭\"，暗示稻农依次单独对付每个敌人；\n原文恰相反，是让两个敌人同台\"互相消耗\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nto harness one foe against the other ↔ Using one evil to combat the other\n预判是\"以一敌制一敌\"，D 的 Using one evil to combat the other 与 harness one\nfoe against the other 在动作结构与对象数量上完全对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn about \"Project Neurice\"?", "options": ["A) Its goals will have to be realized at a cost.", "B) It aims to increase the yield of Spanish rice.", "C) Its immediate priority is to bring the pest under control.", "D) It tries to kill the snails with the help of climate change."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about \"Project Neurice\" 问对某事物的客观了解，对应原文一处或几处\n描述。\n【拆句】题干结构 What do we learn about \"Project Neurice\" 是一个间接疑问句的标准句式，主\n谓简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】\"The project has two sides,\" says Xavier Serrat, Neurice project manager and researcher at\nthe University of Barcelona, \"the short-term fight against the snail—and a mid- to long-term\nfight against climate change. But the snail has given the project greater urgency.\"\n\"这个项目分两面，\"Neurice 项目经理、巴塞罗那大学研究员 Xavier Serrat 说，\"短期是对\n付蜗牛，中长期是对付气候变化。但蜗牛已让这一项目变得更为紧迫。\"\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIts goals will have to be realized at a cost 称项目目标必须\"以某种代价\"实现，原文从\n未谈成本/代价问题。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt aims to increase the yield of Spanish rice 把项目目标改成\"提高西班牙水稻产量\"，\n原文目标是\"培育抗盐品种\"，提到产量数据只是 120 million kilograms of rice a year\n的背景介绍，并不是项目目标。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe short-term fight against the snail ↔\nIts immediate priority is to bring the pest under control\nBut the snail has given the project greater urgency ↔ Its immediate priority\nC 同时对应 short-term fight against the snail 的短期定位与 greater urgency 的紧迫\n级别，符合预判\"当下首要任务是控蜗\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt tries to kill the snails with the help of climate change 把气候变化说成\"杀蜗的帮\n手\"，原文 a mid- to long-term fight against climate change 明确把气候变化列为项\n目要对付的敌人，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Neurice project manager say about the giant apple snail?", "options": ["A) It can survive only on southern European wetlands.", "B) It will invade other rice-growing regions of Europe.", "C) It multiplies at a speed beyond human imagination.", "D) It was introduced into the rice fields on purpose."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does Neurice project manager say about the giant apple snail 直接问某人关于\n某事的原话，是观点引述类细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does Neurice project manager say，后接介词短语 about the giant\napple snail 限定话题范围；陈述对象明确，无嵌套从句。\n【定位】But the snail continues its march to new territory, says Serrat. \"The question is not whether it\nwill reach other rice-growing areas of Europe, but when.\"\n但蜗牛仍在向新地盘扩张，Serrat 说。\"问题不是它会不会蔓延到欧洲其他水稻产区，而是\n何时蔓延过去。\"\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nIt can survive only on southern European wetlands 用 only 把蜗牛活动范围限死在\n南欧湿地，原文只说 For now, the giant apple snail's presence in Europe is limited\nto the Ebro Delta 是\"目前\"的暂时状况，并未说它\"只能\"生存于此。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThe question is not whether it will reach other rice-growing areas of Europe, but when ↔\nIt will invade other rice-growing regions of Europe\n预判是\"扩散必然发生\"，B 的 will invade 把这一必然性直接落到\"将会入侵欧洲其他水\n稻产区\"，与定位句完全等值。\nC) 无中生有\nIt multiplies at a speed beyond human imagination 谈\"繁殖速度惊人\"，原文从未提\n到蜗牛繁殖率/速度的任何描述。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt was introduced into the rice fields on purpose 称\"有意引入\"，原文 the snails\nwere accidentally introduced 明确说\"被意外引入\"，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the ultimate goal of the EU-funded program?", "options": ["A) Cultivating ideal salt-resistant rice varieties.", "B) Increasing the absorbency of the Spanish rice.", "C) Introducing Spanish rice to the rest of Europe.", "D) Popularizing the rice crossbreeding technology."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the ultimate goal of the EU-funded program 直问项目目标，原文末段一般会有\n目的状语/不定式直接呼应。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is the ultimate goal of the EU-funded program，of 短语限定 goal 所属\n对象；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】As an EU-funded effort, the search for salt-tolerant varieties of rice is taking place in all three\ncountries. Each team is crossbreeding a local European short-grain rice with a long-grain\nAsian variety that carries the salt-resistant gene. The scientists are breeding successive\ngenerations to arrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance but retain about 97 percent of\nthe European rice genome.\n作为一项欧盟资助的研究，三国都在寻找耐盐水稻品种。各团队都把本地的欧洲短粒米与一\n种带耐盐基因的亚洲长粒米进行杂交。科学家通过逐代繁育，最终要培育出既具备耐盐性、\n又保留约 97% 欧洲水稻基因组的品种。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nto arrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance but retain about 97 percent of the European\nrice genome\n↔ Cultivating ideal salt-resistant rice varieties\nthe search for salt-tolerant varieties of rice ↔ salt-resistant rice varieties\n预判为\"培育耐盐稻\"，A 的 Cultivating ideal salt-resistant rice varieties 与原文 to\narrive at varieties that incorporate salt tolerance 在动作（培育/获得）与对象（耐盐\n品种）上均等值。\nB) 偷换概念\nIncreasing the absorbency of the Spanish rice 把目标说成\"提升西班牙水稻的吸水\n性\"，原文只在 can withstand the increasing salinity without losing the\nabsorbency 提到\"不能因此丧失吸水性\"，是保底条件，不是要去提升的目标。\nC) 无中生有\nIntroducing Spanish rice to the rest of Europe 称\"把西班牙水稻引入欧洲其他地\n区\"，原文 in all three countries 仅说三国共同研究，并未谈\"推广西班牙米\"。\nD) 偷换概念\nPopularizing the rice crossbreeding technology 把\"杂交技术普及\"当目标，原文\nEach team is crossbreeding… 说明杂交只是培育新品种的手段，目标不是技术本身。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about photo-taking in the past?", "options": ["A) It was a painstaking effort for recording life's major events.", "B) It was a luxury that only a few wealthy people could enjoy.", "C) It was a good way to preserve one's precious images.", "D) It was a skill that required lots of practice to master."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about photo-taking in the past? 问的是作者就\"过去拍照\"陈\n述了什么事实，无 infer/imply/suggest 等推断信号词。\n【拆句】Q51 是规范疑问句，主干 What does the author say，about photo-taking in the past\n作 say 的状语宾语；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Photography was once an expensive, laborious ordeal reserved for life's greatest milestones.\n摄影曾经是一桩昂贵、费力的苦差事，专留给人生最重大的里程碑时刻。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nlaborious ordeal ↔ painstaking effort\nreserved for life's greatest milestones ↔ for recording life's major events\n预判的两层意思\"费力\"和\"为人生大事\"在选项 A 里被 painstaking effort 和 for\nrecording life's major events 同时覆盖，无其他选项做得到。\nB) 过度推断\n原文 expensive 只说\"昂贵\"，并未说\"只有少数富人 (only a few wealthy people) 才能\n享受\"——奢侈品属性是选项添加的。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文用 laborious ordeal（费力的苦差事）做负向描述，选项 C 却把过去的拍照说成是\na good way to preserve one's precious images（保存珍贵影像的好办法），与原文\n情感色彩相反。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只说昂贵、费力、专为大事，对\"是否需要练习掌握\"未置一词。a skill that\nrequired lots of practice to master 是无中生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Kristin Diehl conducted a series of experiments on photo-taking to find out _______.", "options": ["A) What kind of pleasure it would actually bring to photo-takers", "B) Whether people enjoyed it when they did sightseeing", "C) How it could help to enrich people's life experiences", "D) Whether it prevented people enjoying what they were doing"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】conducted a series of experiments on photo-taking to find out 是不完整陈述句，待补\n全的是实验目的，答案必为文中明示的实验动因。\n【拆句】Q52 是带空位的不完整陈述：主语 Kristin Diehl + 谓语 conducted + 宾语 a series of\nexperiments，介词短语 on photo-taking 作定语，不定式 to find out _ 表目的；空位处\n待填实验目的的宾语从句。\n【定位】Diehl and her fellow researchers wanted to find out if that was true, so they embarked on a\nseries of nine experiments in the lab and in the field testing people's enjoyment in the\npresence or absence of a camera.\nDiehl 和她的研究伙伴想要查清那种说法是否成立，于是着手开展了一系列共九项实验，在\n实验室与现场测试人们在有相机和无相机时的愉悦感。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文实验初衷是检验负面说法，what kind of pleasure it would actually bring 把研究\n目的偷换成正向探寻\"种类\"，方向完全反了。\nB) 以偏概全\n观光只是 P5 列举的多个实验场景之一，不是研究的总目的。把全研究目的窄化到\"观光\n时是否享受\"是以偏概全。\nC) 答非所问\nhow it could help to enrich people's life experiences 已经预设拍照有积极作用并追\n问\"如何起作用\"，而原文的研究意图是中性验证\"是否打断\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwanted to find out if that was true ↔\nwhether it prevented people enjoying what they were doing\ninterrupt the experience ↔ prevented people enjoying what they were doing\n把 if that was true 的 that 回指还原为 interrupt the experience，只有 D 把\"打断享\n受\"这一层语义保留下来；其他三项都丢了 that 的回指。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do the results of Diehl's experiments show about people taking pictures?", "options": ["A) They are distracted from what they are doing.", "B) They can better remember what they see or do.", "C) They are more absorbed in what catches their eye.", "D) They can have a better understanding of the world."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do the results of Diehl's experiments show about people taking pictures? 直问\n实验结果\"显示了什么\"，是对原文结论句的同义改写。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 What do the results show，介词短语 of Diehl's experiments 修饰 results，\nabout people taking pictures 作 show 的宾语补语；about 后接动名词复合结构 people\ntaking pictures，意为\"关于拍照者\"。\n【定位】What we find is you actually look at the world slightly differently, because you're looking for\nthings you want to capture, that you may want to hang onto. That gets people more engaged\nin the experience, and they tend to enjoy it more.\n我们发现的是：你其实会以略不同的视角看世界，因为你在寻找想抓拍、想留住的东西。这\n让人在体验中更投入，也更享受。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\ndistracted from what they are doing 与原文 more engaged in the experience 情感\n极性完全相反；原文是\"更投入\"，选项却是\"被分散注意力\"。\nB) 无中生有\n原文 P4 只讲\"看世界的方式不同+更投入\"，未提任何\"记忆/回忆\"的维度。better\nremember what they see or do 是无中生有。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmore engaged in the experience ↔ more absorbed in what catches their eye\nthings you want to capture ↔ what catches their eye\n预判的\"投入\"和\"想抓拍的事物\"两层意思在选项 C 中被 more absorbed 与 what\ncatches their eye 一一对应覆盖。\nD) 过度推断\nyou actually look at the world slightly differently 只说\"略不同地看\"，没说\"更懂\"。\nhave a better understanding of the world 把视觉层面的差异放大到认知层面，幅度\n过大。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is found about museum visitors with the aid of eye-tracking glasses?", "options": ["A) They come out with better photographs of the exhibits.", "B) They focus more on the exhibits when taking pictures.", "C) They have a better view of what are on display.", "D) They follow the historical events more easily."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is found about museum visitors with the aid of eye-tracking glasses? 直问\"借助\n眼动眼镜对博物馆参观者发现了什么\"，答案是定位段的客观陈述。\n【拆句】Q54 被动句：What is found 主干，about museum visitors 限定对象，with the aid of\neye-tracking glasses 是表手段的伴随状语；三层介词短语堆叠但语义清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】It works for things as boring as archaeological museums, where people were given eye-\ntracking glasses and instructed either to take photos or not. \"People look longer at things they\nwant to photograph,\" Diehl says. They report liking the exhibits more, too.\n即便是无趣如考古博物馆也同样奏效：参与者被发给眼动追踪眼镜，并被指令要么拍照、要\n么不拍。\"人们对自己想拍的东西注视更久，\"Diehl 说道。他们也报告说更喜欢这些展品。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\ncome out with better photographs 谈的是照片成果优劣，而原文测的是注视时长与\n喜爱度，与照片质量无关。\nB) ✓ 正确\nPeople look longer at things they want to photograph ↔\nfocus more on the exhibits when taking pictures\nthings they want to photograph ↔ exhibits when taking pictures\n预判中的\"对想拍的看得更久\"在 B 选项中由 focus more 和 exhibits 准确对应，其他三\n项都在概念层走偏。\nC) 偷换概念\nbetter view of what are on display 谈的是物理或视觉上的\"视野更佳\"，而原文谈的是\n注视时长。两者一是 view（视野/景观），一是 look at（注视）——一字之差，所指\n不同。\nD) 无中生有\nfollow the historical events more easily\"更容易跟上历史事件\"是认知/理解层面的描\n述，而原文只测\"注视时长\"与\"喜爱度\"。本选项还把\"博物馆=历史馆\"的刻板印象绑了进\n来，但原文 archaeological（考古的）不等同于 historical events（历史事件）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "They follow the historical events more easily.", "options": ["A) It is better to make plans before taking photos.", "B) Mental photos can be as beautiful as snapshots.", "C) Photographers can derive great joy from the click of the camera.", "D) Even the very thought of taking a photo can have a positive effect."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】题干因疑似录入异常无法直接定题型，但选项 Even the very thought of taking a photo\ncan have a positive effect. 含 even 这一让步推断信号，且对应末段实验拓展性结论，按\n推断题处理最稳妥。\n【拆句】Q55 的字面 They follow the historical events more easily. 是陈述句而非疑问句或不完整\n陈述，明显与选项体例不匹配，按异常处理；逐字解析其语法无意义。\n【定位】Was it the satisfying click of the camera? The physical act of the snap? No, they found; just\nthe act of planning to take a photo—and not actually taking it—had the same joy-boosting\neffect. \"If you want to take mental photos, that works the same way,\" Diehl says. \"Thinking\nabout what you would want to photograph also gets you more engaged.\"\n是相机咔嚓声的满足感吗？还是按下快门那个动作？不是，他们发现：仅仅是\"打算拍照\"这\n一念头——哪怕并未真拍——就同样带来提升愉悦的效果。\"如果你想拍\"心理照\"，效果也\n一样，\"Diehl 说。\"光是去想自己想拍什么，就能让你更投入。\"\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 planning to take a photo 是\"打算拍\"这一念头本身（与\"真拍\"对照），并不是\"拍\n前做规划\"这一行为。It is better to make plans before taking photos. 把\"打算拍\"窄\n化为\"做计划\"，且加了\"更好\"这一比较级判断。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文谈\"心理照与实拍效果相同\"，但相同的是 joy-boosting effect（提升愉悦的效\n果），不是美学层面的\"漂亮\"。Mental photos can be as beautiful as snapshots. 把\n效果维度偷换成美感维度。\nC) 与原文相反\nWas it the satisfying click of the camera?...No, they found——原文用 No 直接否\n认\"咔嚓声是喜悦来源\"，而选项 C 却说 Photographers can derive great joy from the\nclick of the camera，正话反说。\nD) ✓ 正确\njust the act of planning to take a photo—and not actually taking it—had the same joy-boosting\neffect\n↔ Even the very thought of taking a photo can have a positive effect.\nThinking about what you would want to photograph also gets you more engaged ↔\nEven the very thought of taking a photo can have a positive effect.\n定位句的核心让步 and not actually taking it 只有选项 D 中的 Even...the very\nthought 把\"哪怕仅是念头\"这一层完整表达出来。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
